('The magnification produced by a lens is $m = v/u$. For a real inverted image formed by a convex lens, $m$ is', 'Positive and greater than 1', 'Negative', 'Always $+1$', 'Zero', 'option2', 'For a real image: $v > 0$, $u < 0$, so $m = v/u < 0$ (negative means inverted).', 'p_magnification_prism', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('When white light passes through a glass prism, the colour that deviates the most is', 'Red', 'Green', 'Yellow', 'Violet', 'option4', 'Violet has the shortest wavelength and highest refractive index in glass, so it deviates the most.', 'p_magnification_prism', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The angle of minimum deviation for a prism occurs when', 'The ray is parallel to the base', 'The angle of incidence is $90°$', 'The ray passes symmetrically through the prism ($i = e$)', 'The angle of incidence is zero', 'option3', 'At minimum deviation, the refracted ray inside the prism is parallel to the base, and $i = e$ (symmetric passage).', 'p_magnification_prism', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two thin lenses of powers $+5\;\text{D}$ and $-3\;\text{D}$ in contact have combined power', '$+8\;\text{D}$', '$+2\;\text{D}$', '$-2\;\text{D}$', '$+15\;\text{D}$', 'option2', '$P = P_1 + P_2 = 5 + (-3) = +2\;\text{D}$.', 'p_magnification_prism', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The deviation produced by a thin prism of angle $A$ and refractive index $n$ is', '$(n+1)A$', '$(n-1)A$', '$nA$', '$A/n$', 'option2', 'For a thin prism: $\delta = (n-1)A$ (independent of angle of incidence for small $A$).', 'p_magnification_prism', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The refractive index of a prism in terms of angle $A$ and minimum deviation $\delta_m$ is', '$\sin(A/2)/\sin((A+\delta_m)/2)$', '$\sin((A+\delta_m)/2)/\sin(A/2)$', '$\cos((A+\delta_m)/2)/\cos(A/2)$', '$\tan(A/2)/\tan(\delta_m/2)$', 'option2', '$n = \sin((A+\delta_m)/2)/\sin(A/2)$.', 'p_magnification_prism', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A concave lens of focal length $20\;\text{cm}$ has power', '$+5\;\text{D}$', '$-5\;\text{D}$', '$+20\;\text{D}$', '$-0.05\;\text{D}$', 'option2', '$P = 1/f = 1/(-0.20) = -5\;\text{D}$ (negative for concave/diverging lens).', 'p_magnification_prism', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An equilateral glass prism ($n = 1.5$) has minimum deviation', '$30°$', '$37°$', '$45°$', '$60°$', 'option2', '$n = \sin((A+\delta_m)/2)/\sin(A/2)$. $1.5 = \sin((60+\delta_m)/2)/\sin 30°$. $\sin((60+\delta_m)/2) = 0.75$. $(60+\delta_m)/2 = \sin^{-1}(0.75) \approx 48.6°$. $\delta_m \approx 37.2°$.', 'p_magnification_prism', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A prism of angle $60°$ and refractive index $\sqrt{2}$ has minimum deviation', '$60°$', '$45°$', '$30°$', '$90°$', 'option3', '$n = \sin((A+\delta_m)/2)/\sin(A/2)$. $\sqrt{2} = \sin((60+\delta_m)/2)/\sin 30°$. $\sin((60+\delta_m)/2) = \sqrt{2}/2$. $(60+\delta_m)/2 = 45°$. $\delta_m = 30°$.', 'p_magnification_prism', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Chromatic aberration in lenses is caused by', 'Diffraction at the aperture', 'Spherical shape of the lens', 'Variation of focal length with wavelength (dispersion)', 'Reflection losses', 'option3', 'Different wavelengths have different refractive indices, causing different focal lengths — this is chromatic aberration.', 'p_magnification_prism', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An achromatic doublet is made by combining', 'Two concave lenses', 'Two identical convex lenses', 'A convex lens of crown glass with a concave lens of flint glass', 'A lens and a mirror', 'option3', 'An achromatic doublet uses two lenses of different dispersive powers to cancel chromatic aberration while maintaining net power.', 'p_magnification_prism', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The angular dispersion produced by a thin prism of angle $A$ is', '$A(\mu_V - \mu_R)$', '$A(\mu_Y - 1)$', '$A/(\mu_V - \mu_R)$', '$(\mu_V - \mu_R)/A$', 'option1', 'Angular dispersion $= \delta_V - \delta_R = (\mu_V - 1)A - (\mu_R - 1)A = (\mu_V - \mu_R)A$.', 'p_magnification_prism', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A thin prism ($A = 6°$, $n = 1.5$) is combined with another thin prism to produce dispersion without deviation. If the second prism has $n'' = 1.75$, its angle is', '$4°$', '$-4°$', '$3°$', '$8°$', 'option2', 'For zero deviation: $(n_1-1)A_1 + (n_2-1)A_2 = 0$. $0.5 \times 6 + 0.75 \times A_2 = 0$. $A_2 = -4°$ (opposite orientation).', 'p_magnification_prism', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A convex lens of focal length $f$ produces an image of the Sun. The diameter of the image is proportional to', '$f$', '$1/f$', '$f^2$', '$\sqrt{f}$', 'option1', 'The Sun subtends angle $\alpha$ at the lens. Image diameter $= f\alpha$, so it is proportional to $f$.', 'p_magnification_prism', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The dispersive power of a prism material is defined as', '$\omega = \mu_V - \mu_R$', '$\omega = \mu_Y/(\mu_V - \mu_R)$', '$\omega = (\mu_V + \mu_R)/2$', '$\omega = (\mu_V - \mu_R)/(\mu_Y - 1)$', 'option4', 'Dispersive power $\omega = (\mu_V - \mu_R)/(\mu_Y - 1)$ where $V$, $R$, $Y$ denote violet, red, and yellow (mean).', 'p_magnification_prism', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A ray of white light enters a prism at minimum deviation for yellow light. The red and violet rays emerge', 'Neither deviates', 'Red deviates more than violet', 'Both deviate equally', 'Violet deviates more than red (angular spread)', 'option4', 'At minimum deviation for yellow, violet ($n$ higher) deviates more and red ($n$ lower) deviates less, producing a spectrum.', 'p_magnification_prism', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two thin lenses of focal lengths $+20\;\text{cm}$ and $-40\;\text{cm}$ are separated by $10\;\text{cm}$. The equivalent focal length is', '$40\;\text{cm}$', '$20\;\text{cm}$', '$80/3\;\text{cm}$', '$60\;\text{cm}$', 'option3', '$1/f = 1/f_1 + 1/f_2 - d/(f_1 f_2) = 1/20 - 1/40 + 10/800 = (4-2+1)/80 = 3/80$. $f = 80/3 \approx 26.7\;\text{cm}$.', 'p_magnification_prism', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A glass prism ($n = 1.5$, $A = 4°$) and a water prism ($n = 1.33$) are combined for zero deviation. The angle of the water prism is', '$-4°$', '$+6.06°$', '$-6.06°$', '$+4°$', 'option3', '$(n_1-1)A_1 + (n_2-1)A_2 = 0$. $0.5 \times 4 + 0.33 \times A_2 = 0$. $A_2 = -2/0.33 = -6.06°$.', 'p_magnification_prism', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('For a prism at minimum deviation, the relation between angle of incidence $i$, prism angle $A$, and minimum deviation $\delta_m$ is', '$i = A + \delta_m$', '$i = A/2$', '$i = \delta_m/2$', '$i = (A + \delta_m)/2$', 'option4', 'At minimum deviation: $i = e$ and $r_1 = r_2 = A/2$. Also $\delta_m = 2i - A$, giving $i = (A + \delta_m)/2$.', 'p_magnification_prism', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_optical_instruments (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('The magnifying power of a simple microscope (magnifying glass) for the image at the near point is', '$1 + D/f$', '$D/f$', '$f/D$', '$D + f$', 'option1', '$M = 1 + D/f$ where $D = 25\;\text{cm}$ (least distance of distinct vision) and $f$ is the focal length.', 'p_optical_instruments', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An astronomical telescope in normal adjustment has magnifying power', '$f_o + f_e$', '$f_e/f_o$', '$-f_o/f_e$', '$f_o \times f_e$', 'option3', '$M = -f_o/f_e$ (negative sign indicates inverted image). Length of telescope $= f_o + f_e$.', 'p_optical_instruments', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The length of an astronomical telescope in normal adjustment is', '$f_o + f_e$', '$f_o - f_e$', '$f_o \times f_e$', '$f_o/f_e$', 'option1', 'In normal adjustment (final image at infinity): tube length $= f_o + f_e$.', 'p_optical_instruments', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A compound microscope uses', 'Two convex lenses: a short focal length objective and a longer focal length eyepiece', 'Two concave lenses', 'One convex and one concave lens', 'A single convex lens', 'option1', 'The objective (short $f$) forms a magnified real image, which the eyepiece (longer $f$) further magnifies.', 'p_optical_instruments', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The resolving power of a telescope increases with', 'Increasing focal length of the eyepiece', 'Increasing aperture of the objective', 'Decreasing aperture', 'Increasing magnification only', 'option2', 'Resolving power $\propto D/\lambda$ where $D$ is the aperture diameter. Larger aperture resolves finer details.', 'p_optical_instruments', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A reflecting telescope uses a concave mirror as the objective instead of a lens. An advantage is', 'No chromatic aberration', 'Higher magnification always', 'Smaller size', 'No spherical aberration', 'option1', 'Mirrors do not suffer from chromatic aberration (no dispersion). They can also be made larger than lenses.', 'p_optical_instruments', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The human eye can focus objects at different distances by changing the', 'Size of the pupil only', 'Focal length of the eye lens (accommodation)', 'Distance between lens and retina', 'Refractive index of the lens', 'option2', 'The ciliary muscles change the curvature of the eye lens, adjusting its focal length (accommodation).', 'p_optical_instruments', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An astronomical telescope has $f_o = 100\;\text{cm}$ and $f_e = 5\;\text{cm}$. Its magnifying power in normal adjustment is', '$+105$', '$+20$', '$-105$', '$-20$', 'option4', '$M = -f_o/f_e = -100/5 = -20$ (inverted image).', 'p_optical_instruments', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The magnifying power of a compound microscope is approximately', '$-(L/f_o)(D/f_e)$ where $L$ is the tube length', '$f_o f_e/D$', '$D/(f_o + f_e)$', '$L \times D$', 'option1', '$M \approx -(L/f_o)(D/f_e)$ for the final image at the near point, where $L$ is the distance between the lenses.', 'p_optical_instruments', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A person with myopia (near-sightedness) needs', 'No correction', 'A convex (converging) lens', 'A cylindrical lens', 'A concave (diverging) lens for correction', 'option4', 'Myopia: far point is closer than infinity. A concave lens diverges rays so they appear to come from the far point.', 'p_optical_instruments', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A person with hypermetropia (far-sightedness) needs', 'Surgery only', 'A concave lens', 'A prism', 'A convex (converging) lens for correction', 'option4', 'Hypermetropia: near point is farther than $25\;\text{cm}$. A convex lens converges rays to bring the near point closer.', 'p_optical_instruments', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A magnifying glass of focal length $5\;\text{cm}$ has magnifying power (image at near point, $D = 25\;\text{cm}$)', '$4$', '$5$', '$6$', '$25$', 'option3', '$M = 1 + D/f = 1 + 25/5 = 6$.', 'p_optical_instruments', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The angular magnification of a telescope when the final image is at the near point is', '$-D/f_e$', '$-f_o/f_e$', '$f_o + f_e$', '$-(f_o/f_e)(1 + f_e/D)$', 'option4', 'When the final image is at the near point: $M = -(f_o/f_e)(1 + f_e/D)$, which is slightly larger than in normal adjustment.', 'p_optical_instruments', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The limit of resolution of the human eye is approximately', '$1$ second of arc', '$1°$', '$1$ minute of arc', '$0.1°$', 'option3', 'The human eye can resolve two points separated by about $1''$ (one minute of arc), corresponding to $\sim 0.1\;\text{mm}$ at $25\;\text{cm}$.', 'p_optical_instruments', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A Galilean telescope uses', 'A convex objective and a concave eyepiece', 'Two convex lenses', 'Two concave lenses', 'A concave objective and convex eyepiece', 'option1', 'Galilean telescope: convex objective + concave eyepiece. It produces an erect image and has shorter tube length ($f_o - |f_e|$).', 'p_optical_instruments', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The resolving power of a microscope is proportional to', '$D/f$', '$\lambda/(n\sin\theta)$', '$f/D$', '$n\sin\theta/\lambda$ where $n\sin\theta$ is the numerical aperture', 'option4', 'Resolving power $= 2n\sin\theta/\lambda$. Higher NA and shorter wavelength improve resolution.', 'p_optical_instruments', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A compound microscope has objective $f_o = 1\;\text{cm}$ and eyepiece $f_e = 5\;\text{cm}$, separated by $20\;\text{cm}$. The approximate magnification (image at infinity) is', '$-45$', '$-75$', '$-15$', '$-100$', 'option2', 'Tube length $L = 20 - f_o - f_e = 20 - 1 - 5 = 14\;\text{cm}$ (approx image distance from objective minus $f_e$). $M \approx -(L \times D)/(f_o \times f_e) = -(14 \times 25)/(1 \times 5) = -70$. Closest is $-75$.', 'p_optical_instruments', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An astronomical telescope is focused on a distant object. If the entire telescope is immersed in water, the magnifying power', 'Remains unchanged', 'Increases', 'Decreases', 'Becomes zero', 'option1', 'Magnifying power $= f_o/f_e$. Both focal lengths change by the same factor in water, so the ratio (and hence magnification) remains unchanged.', 'p_optical_instruments', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The minimum distance between an object and its real image formed by a convex lens of focal length $f$ is', '$f$', '$2f$', '$4f$', '$3f$', 'option3', 'Let object be at $u$ from lens. $v = uf/(u-f)$. Distance $d = v - u = uf/(u-f) - u = u^2/(u-f) - u(u-f)/(u-f) = uf/(u-f)$. Minimizing $d = u + uf/(u-f)$: $dd/du = 0$ gives $u = 2f$, $v = 2f$, $d_{min} = 4f$.', 'p_optical_instruments', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a terrestrial telescope, an additional erecting lens is used to make the final image', 'Brighter', 'Erect', 'Larger', 'At infinity', 'option2', 'An astronomical telescope gives an inverted image. A terrestrial telescope adds a third convex lens to erect the image, at the cost of increased tube length.', 'p_optical_instruments', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Rayleigh criterion for the minimum resolvable angle of a telescope with aperture $D$ at wavelength $\lambda$ is', '$1.22\lambda/D$', '$\lambda/D$', '$D/\lambda$', '$1.22D/\lambda$', 'option1', 'Rayleigh criterion: $\theta_{min} = 1.22\lambda/D$ radians.', 'p_optical_instruments', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_wave_optics_huygens (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('According to Huygens'' principle, every point on a wavefront acts as a source of', 'Longitudinal waves', 'Secondary wavelets', 'Standing waves', 'Electromagnetic radiation', 'option2', 'Huygens'' principle: each point on a wavefront is a source of secondary spherical wavelets. The new wavefront is the envelope of these wavelets.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A plane wavefront incident on a convex lens emerges as', 'A plane wavefront', 'A spherical converging wavefront', 'A cylindrical wavefront', 'A diverging wavefront', 'option2', 'A convex lens converts a plane wavefront into a spherical wavefront converging at the focus.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The wavefronts from a point source at a large distance are approximately', 'Spherical', 'Plane', 'Cylindrical', 'Elliptical', 'option2', 'At large distances from a point source, the radius of curvature is so large that the wavefronts are effectively plane.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Huygens'' principle can explain', 'Only reflection', 'Both reflection and refraction of light', 'Only refraction', 'Neither reflection nor refraction', 'option2', 'Huygens'' construction successfully derives the laws of both reflection and refraction.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('When a plane wavefront passes through a narrow slit, the emerging wavefront is', 'Still plane', 'Cylindrical (spreading out)', 'Spherical converging', 'Unchanged', 'option2', 'Diffraction at a narrow slit causes the wavefront to spread out, becoming approximately cylindrical.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In Huygens'' construction for refraction, the bending of light occurs because', 'The speed of light is different in different media', 'Light is a particle', 'The wavelength does not change', 'The frequency changes', 'option1', 'Refraction occurs because light travels at different speeds in different media. The wavelength changes but frequency remains constant.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The wavefronts from a line source are', 'Spherical', 'Plane', 'Cylindrical', 'Conical', 'option3', 'A line source produces cylindrical wavefronts (expanding outward from the line).', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Using Huygens'' principle, when light goes from a denser to a rarer medium, the refracted wavefront', 'Bends towards the normal', 'Bends away from the normal because the speed increases', 'Does not bend', 'Reverses direction', 'option2', 'In the rarer medium, light travels faster. The part of the wavefront entering first travels farther, causing the wavefront to tilt away from the normal.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The speed of light in a medium of refractive index $n$ is $v = c/n$. When light enters glass ($n = 1.5$) from air, its wavelength', 'Doubles', 'Increases by factor $1.5$', 'Remains the same', 'Decreases by factor $1.5$', 'option4', '$\lambda_{medium} = \lambda_0/n$. Wavelength decreases. Frequency stays constant.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A concave mirror converts an incident plane wavefront into', 'A diverging spherical wavefront', 'A converging spherical wavefront', 'Another plane wavefront', 'A cylindrical wavefront', 'option2', 'A concave mirror reflects a plane wavefront into a spherical wavefront converging at the focus.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In Huygens'' construction for reflection, the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection because', 'The speed of light does not change upon reflection', 'The wavelength changes', 'The frequency changes', 'The medium changes', 'option1', 'Since the reflected wave travels in the same medium at the same speed, geometric construction shows $\angle i = \angle r$.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The phase velocity of light in a medium is', '$c/n^2$', '$cn$', '$c$', '$c/n$', 'option4', 'Phase velocity $v_p = c/n$ where $n$ is the refractive index.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A spherical wavefront from a point source at distance $d$ from a convex lens of focal length $f$ emerges as', 'A spherical wavefront converging at $v$ given by $1/v - 1/(-d) = 1/f$', 'A plane wavefront always', 'A diverging wavefront always', 'An unchanged spherical wavefront', 'option1', 'The lens modifies the curvature of the wavefront according to the lens equation.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A prism deviates a plane wavefront because', 'The prism reflects the wavefront', 'The prism absorbs part of the wavefront', 'The frequency changes inside the prism', 'Different parts of the wavefront travel different optical path lengths through the prism', 'option4', 'The thicker part of the prism introduces more optical path, retarding that part of the wavefront and causing deviation.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Huygens'' principle could not originally explain', 'Wavefront propagation', 'Reflection', 'Refraction', 'The absence of a backward wave', 'option4', 'Huygens had to postulate (without proof) that the secondary wavelets do not produce a backward wave. This was later explained by Kirchhoff.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The optical path length through a medium of thickness $t$ and refractive index $n$ is', '$nt$', '$t/n$', '$t$', '$n/t$', 'option1', 'Optical path $= nt$. It represents the equivalent distance in vacuum that would produce the same phase change.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A glass slab of thickness $t$ and refractive index $n$ introduces an additional optical path of', '$(n-1)t$', '$nt$', '$t/n$', '$(n+1)t$', 'option1', 'Extra optical path compared to air $= nt - t = (n-1)t$.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('When a plane wavefront passes through a thin lens, the emerging wavefront is spherical because', 'Diffraction at the lens edge', 'The lens absorbs the edges', 'The lens introduces a quadratic phase variation across the wavefront', 'The lens changes the frequency', 'option3', 'A thin lens introduces an optical path difference that varies quadratically with distance from the axis, converting a plane wavefront to spherical.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Fermat''s principle states that light travels between two points along the path that', 'Has maximum optical path', 'Is the shortest distance', 'Takes the least time (or is stationary)', 'Is always a straight line', 'option3', 'Fermat''s principle: light follows the path of least (or stationary) optical path length, which is equivalent to least time.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Fresnel distance $z_F$ for a slit of width $a$ at wavelength $\lambda$ is', '$a/\lambda$', '$\lambda/a^2$', '$a\lambda$', '$a^2/\lambda$', 'option4', 'Fresnel distance $z_F = a^2/\lambda$. Beyond this distance, diffraction effects dominate over ray optics.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A zone plate works on the principle of', 'Diffraction (blocking alternate Fresnel zones)', 'Refraction', 'Total internal reflection', 'Polarization', 'option1', 'A zone plate blocks alternate Fresnel zones so that the remaining zones constructively interfere, focusing light like a lens.', 'p_wave_optics_huygens', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_interference_young (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('For sustained interference, the two sources must be', 'Of different frequencies', 'Coherent (same frequency and constant phase difference)', 'Very far apart', 'Of different amplitudes', 'option2', 'Coherent sources maintain a constant phase relationship, which is essential for a stable interference pattern.', 'p_interference_young', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In Young''s double-slit experiment, the fringe width $\beta$ is given by', '$\lambda d/D$', '$d\lambda D$', '$\lambda D/d$', '$D/(d\lambda)$', 'option3', '$\beta = \lambda D/d$ where $\lambda$ = wavelength, $D$ = screen distance, $d$ = slit separation.', 'p_interference_young', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Constructive interference occurs when the path difference is', 'An odd multiple of $\lambda/2$', 'An integral multiple of $\lambda$', 'Zero only', 'Any random value', 'option2', 'Constructive: path difference $= n\lambda$ ($n = 0, 1, 2, ...$). Destructive: $(2n+1)\lambda/2$.', 'p_interference_young', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The central fringe in Young''s experiment is', 'Always dark', 'Always bright', 'Sometimes bright, sometimes dark', 'Missing', 'option2', 'At the central point, path difference is zero, so constructive interference always occurs (bright fringe).', 'p_interference_young', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the slit separation in Young''s experiment is halved, the fringe width', 'Halves', 'Doubles', 'Remains the same', 'Quadruples', 'option2', '$\beta = \lambda D/d$. If $d \to d/2$: $\beta \to 2\beta$ (doubles).', 'p_interference_young', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Interference demonstrates the _____ nature of light.', 'Particle', 'Wave', 'Dual', 'Corpuscular', 'option2', 'Interference is a purely wave phenomenon — it cannot be explained by the particle model of light.', 'p_interference_young', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In Young''s experiment, if white light is used, the central fringe is', 'Missing', 'Coloured', 'Dark', 'White', 'option4', 'At the centre, all wavelengths have zero path difference and interfere constructively, giving a white fringe.', 'p_interference_young', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In Young''s experiment with $\lambda = 600\;\text{nm}$, $d = 0.1\;\text{mm}$, $D = 1\;\text{m}$, the fringe width is', '$3\;\text{mm}$', '$6\;\text{mm}$', '$0.6\;\text{mm}$', '$60\;\text{mm}$', 'option2', '$\beta = \lambda D/d = 600 \times 10^{-9} \times 1/(0.1 \times 10^{-3}) = 6 \times 10^{-3}\;\text{m} = 6\;\text{mm}$.', 'p_interference_young', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the entire Young''s experiment is immersed in water ($n = 4/3$), the fringe width', 'Increases by factor $4/3$', 'Remains the same', 'Decreases by factor $4/3$', 'Becomes zero', 'option3', '$\lambda_{water} = \lambda/n$. $\beta'' = \lambda_{water} D/d = \beta/n$. Fringe width decreases by factor $n = 4/3$.', 'p_interference_young', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The intensity at a point in an interference pattern where the phase difference is $\phi$ (equal amplitude sources) is', '$4I_0\cos^2(\phi/2)$', '$2I_0\cos^2(\phi/2)$', '$I_0\cos^2\phi$', '$4I_0\sin^2(\phi/2)$', 'option1', '$I = 4I_0\cos^2(\phi/2)$ where $I_0$ is the intensity from each slit.', 'p_interference_young', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In Young''s experiment, the ratio of maximum to minimum intensity when the slit widths are in ratio $4:1$ is', '$4:1$', '$9:1$', '$16:1$', '$25:1$', 'option2', 'Amplitude ratio $= \sqrt{4}:\sqrt{1} = 2:1$. $I_{max}/I_{min} = (2+1)^2/(2-1)^2 = 9/1$.', 'p_interference_young', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A thin film of thickness $t$ and refractive index $n$ appears bright in reflected light when', '$2nt = (m + 1/2)\lambda$', '$2nt = m\lambda$', '$nt = m\lambda$', '$2t = m\lambda$', 'option1', 'For reflected light (with phase change at one surface): constructive when $2nt = (m + 1/2)\lambda$.', 'p_interference_young', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If one slit in Young''s experiment is covered, the pattern on the screen becomes', 'A set of equally spaced fringes', 'Completely dark', 'Unchanged', 'A single-slit diffraction pattern', 'option4', 'With one slit, there is no two-source interference. The pattern is a single-slit diffraction pattern.', 'p_interference_young', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The path difference at the $n$th bright fringe from the centre in Young''s experiment is', '$n\lambda/2$', '$(2n+1)\lambda/2$', '$n\lambda$', '$(n-1)\lambda$', 'option3', 'Bright fringes occur at path difference $= n\lambda$ ($n = 0, 1, 2, ...$).', 'p_interference_young', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In Young''s experiment, a glass slab of thickness $t$ and refractive index $n$ is placed over one slit. The central fringe shifts by', '$ntD/d$', '$(n-1)tD/d$', '$(n-1)t/d$', '$tD/(nd)$', 'option2', 'Extra path $= (n-1)t$. Shift $= (n-1)t \times D/d = (n-1)tD/d$.', 'p_interference_young', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two coherent sources of intensity $I_1$ and $I_2$ produce interference. The maximum intensity is', '$I_1 + I_2$', '$(\sqrt{I_1} + \sqrt{I_2})^2$', '$2(I_1 + I_2)$', '$4\sqrt{I_1 I_2}$', 'option2', '$I_{max} = (\sqrt{I_1} + \sqrt{I_2})^2 = I_1 + I_2 + 2\sqrt{I_1 I_2}$.', 'p_interference_young', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a biprism experiment, the virtual source separation is $d = 2a(n-1)\alpha$ where $a$ is the distance from the biprism to the source, $n$ is the refractive index, and $\alpha$ is the prism angle. If $a = 20\;\text{cm}$, $n = 1.5$, $\alpha = 1°$, then $d$ is approximately', '$0.35\;\text{mm}$', '$3.5\;\text{mm}$', '$0.035\;\text{mm}$', '$35\;\text{mm}$', 'option2', '$d = 2 \times 20 \times 0.5 \times (\pi/180) = 20 \times 0.01745 = 0.349\;\text{cm} = 3.49\;\text{mm}$.', 'p_interference_young', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In Newton''s rings, the radius of the $n$th dark ring is', '$n\sqrt{\lambda R}$', '$n\lambda R$', '$\sqrt{n\lambda/R}$', '$\sqrt{n\lambda R}$', 'option4', '$r_n = \sqrt{n\lambda R}$ where $R$ is the radius of curvature of the lens.', 'p_interference_young', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The angular width of the central maximum in a single-slit diffraction pattern is $2\lambda/a$. In Young''s double-slit experiment, the number of interference fringes within this central diffraction envelope is approximately', '$2d/a - 1$', '$d/a$', '$a/d$', '$2d/a$', 'option1', 'Number of bright fringes $\approx 2d/a - 1$ (the central diffraction maximum spans $2d/a$ fringe widths, minus the two missing orders at the edges).', 'p_interference_young', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Lloyd''s mirror experiment produces interference fringes. The central fringe is', 'Missing', 'Bright', 'Dark (due to phase change on reflection)', 'Neither dark nor bright', 'option3', 'Reflection from the mirror introduces a $\pi$ phase change, making the central fringe dark (destructive interference).', 'p_interference_young', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In Young''s experiment, if the wavelength is changed from $600\;\text{nm}$ to $400\;\text{nm}$, the fringe width changes by a factor of', '$4/6$', '$3/2$', '$2/3$', '$6/4$', 'option3', '$\beta \propto \lambda$. Ratio $= 400/600 = 2/3$.', 'p_interference_young', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_diffraction (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('Diffraction of light is the phenomenon of', 'Bending of light around obstacles or through narrow openings', 'Splitting of light into colours', 'Reflection of light', 'Absorption of light', 'option1', 'Diffraction is the spreading of waves when they encounter obstacles or apertures comparable to their wavelength.', 'p_diffraction', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Diffraction is most pronounced when the slit width is', 'Much larger than the wavelength', 'Comparable to the wavelength', 'Much smaller than the wavelength', 'Independent of wavelength', 'option2', 'Diffraction effects are significant when the aperture size $a \sim \lambda$.', 'p_diffraction', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In single-slit diffraction, the condition for the first minimum is', '$a\sin\theta = 2\lambda$', '$a\sin\theta = \lambda$', '$a\sin\theta = \lambda/2$', '$a\cos\theta = \lambda$', 'option2', 'First minimum: $a\sin\theta = \lambda$ (path difference of $\lambda$ between the two edges of the slit).', 'p_diffraction', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The central maximum in a single-slit diffraction pattern is', 'Twice as wide as the other maxima', 'Same width as other maxima', 'Half as wide', 'Absent', 'option1', 'The central maximum extends from $-\lambda/a$ to $+\lambda/a$, making it twice the width of secondary maxima.', 'p_diffraction', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Diffraction demonstrates that light is a', 'Particle', 'Wave', 'Neither wave nor particle', 'Mixture of particles and waves', 'option2', 'Diffraction is a wave phenomenon that cannot be explained by the particle model.', 'p_diffraction', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The angular width of the central maximum in single-slit diffraction is', '$\lambda/a$', '$2\lambda/a$', '$a/\lambda$', '$\lambda/(2a)$', 'option2', 'Angular half-width $= \lambda/a$. Full angular width of central maximum $= 2\lambda/a$.', 'p_diffraction', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('As the slit width decreases in single-slit diffraction, the central maximum', 'Becomes narrower', 'Becomes wider', 'Disappears', 'Remains unchanged', 'option2', 'Width $\propto \lambda/a$. Smaller $a$ means wider central maximum (more spreading).', 'p_diffraction', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In single-slit diffraction with slit width $a = 0.1\;\text{mm}$ and $\lambda = 500\;\text{nm}$, the angular position of the first minimum is', '$0.05\;\text{rad}$', '$0.005\;\text{rad}$', '$0.5\;\text{rad}$', '$5 \times 10^{-4}\;\text{rad}$', 'option2', '$\sin\theta \approx \theta = \lambda/a = 500 \times 10^{-9}/(0.1 \times 10^{-3}) = 5 \times 10^{-3} = 0.005\;\text{rad}$.', 'p_diffraction', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The width of the central maximum on a screen at distance $D$ from a single slit of width $a$ is', '$4\lambda D/a$', '$\lambda D/a$', '$\lambda D/(2a)$', '$2\lambda D/a$', 'option4', 'Linear half-width $= D\lambda/a$. Full width $= 2D\lambda/a$.', 'p_diffraction', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The condition for the $n$th secondary maximum in single-slit diffraction is approximately', '$a\sin\theta = (n + 1/2)\lambda$', '$a\sin\theta = n\lambda$', '$a\sin\theta = (2n+1)\lambda$', '$a\sin\theta = n\lambda/2$', 'option1', 'Secondary maxima occur approximately at $a\sin\theta = (n + 1/2)\lambda$ for $n = 1, 2, 3, ...$ (midway between minima).', 'p_diffraction', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The intensity of the first secondary maximum relative to the central maximum in single-slit diffraction is approximately', '$25\%$', '$50\%$', '$4.5\%$', '$10\%$', 'option3', '$I_1/I_0 = [1/(3\pi/2)]^2 \times I_0 = 4/(9\pi^2) \approx 0.045 = 4.5\%$.', 'p_diffraction', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the wavelength of light used in single-slit diffraction is doubled, the width of the central maximum', 'Halves', 'Doubles', 'Remains the same', 'Quadruples', 'option2', 'Width $\propto \lambda$. Doubling $\lambda$ doubles the width.', 'p_diffraction', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In Fraunhofer diffraction, the source and screen are effectively at', 'Finite distances from the slit', 'Infinite distances from the slit (parallel rays)', 'The same location', 'The focal point of the slit', 'option2', 'Fraunhofer diffraction uses plane wavefronts (source and screen at infinity, achieved with lenses in practice).', 'p_diffraction', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A diffraction grating with $N$ slits per mm has grating element $d$ equal to', '$1/N\;\text{mm}$', '$N\;\text{mm}$', '$N\lambda$', '$\lambda/N$', 'option1', 'Grating element (slit spacing) $d = 1/N$ mm.', 'p_diffraction', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The resolving power of a diffraction grating with $N$ total slits in the $m$th order is', '$mN$', '$N/m$', '$m/N$', '$N^2 m$', 'option1', 'Resolving power $= mN$ where $m$ is the order and $N$ is the total number of slits.', 'p_diffraction', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A diffraction grating has $5000$ lines/cm. The maximum order visible for $\lambda = 500\;\text{nm}$ is', '$5$', '$4$', '$3$', '$10$', 'option2', '$d = 1/5000\;\text{cm} = 2 \times 10^{-4}\;\text{cm} = 2\;\mu\text{m}$. $m_{max} = d/\lambda = 2000/500 = 4$.', 'p_diffraction', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a single-slit diffraction pattern, if the slit width equals the wavelength ($a = \lambda$), the first minimum occurs at', '$45°$', '$30°$', '$90°$', '$60°$', 'option3', '$a\sin\theta = \lambda$. $\sin\theta = \lambda/\lambda = 1$. $\theta = 90°$. The entire forward hemisphere is the central maximum.', 'p_diffraction', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The missing orders in a double-slit pattern occur when', 'The interference maximum coincides with a diffraction minimum', 'The slits are too wide', 'The wavelength is too large', 'The screen is too close', 'option1', 'When $d\sin\theta = m\lambda$ (interference max) and $a\sin\theta = n\lambda$ (diffraction min) coincide, the order $m = nd/a$ is missing.', 'p_diffraction', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The angular dispersion of a grating in the $m$th order is', '$d/(m\lambda)$', '$d\cos\theta/m$', '$m\lambda/d$', '$m/(d\cos\theta)$', 'option4', 'From $d\sin\theta = m\lambda$: $d\cos\theta\,d\theta = m\,d\lambda$. Angular dispersion $= d\theta/d\lambda = m/(d\cos\theta)$.', 'p_diffraction', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A circular aperture of diameter $D$ produces a diffraction pattern. The angular radius of the first dark ring (Airy disc) is', '$1.22\lambda/D$', '$\lambda/D$', '$2.44\lambda/D$', '$0.61\lambda/D$', 'option1', 'For a circular aperture: first minimum at $\theta = 1.22\lambda/D$ (Airy pattern).', 'p_diffraction', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Fresnel diffraction differs from Fraunhofer diffraction in that', 'It uses a grating', 'The source or screen (or both) are at finite distance from the aperture', 'It only works with lasers', 'The slit must be very wide', 'option2', 'Fresnel: finite distances (curved wavefronts). Fraunhofer: infinite distances (plane wavefronts, simpler analysis).', 'p_diffraction', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_polarization (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('Polarization of light proves that light is a', 'Longitudinal wave', 'Transverse wave', 'Mechanical wave', 'Sound wave', 'option2', 'Only transverse waves can be polarized. Longitudinal waves cannot exhibit polarization.', 'p_polarization', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Plane-polarized light has vibrations of the electric field', 'In all directions perpendicular to propagation', 'In one particular direction perpendicular to propagation', 'Along the direction of propagation', 'In random directions', 'option2', 'In plane-polarized light, $\vec{E}$ oscillates in a single plane containing the direction of propagation.', 'p_polarization', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Brewster''s angle $\theta_B$ is related to the refractive index $n$ by', '$\tan\theta_B = n$', '$\sin\theta_B = n$', '$\cos\theta_B = n$', '$\tan\theta_B = 1/n$', 'option1', 'Brewster''s law: $\tan\theta_B = n$. At this angle, reflected light is completely polarized.', 'p_polarization', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A Polaroid works by', 'Selective absorption of one component of polarization', 'Reflection', 'Diffraction', 'Refraction', 'option1', 'A Polaroid absorbs the component of light polarized along one direction and transmits the perpendicular component.', 'p_polarization', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('When unpolarized light passes through a single Polaroid, the intensity becomes', '$I_0/2$', '$I_0$', '$I_0/4$', 'Zero', 'option1', 'A single Polaroid transmits half the intensity of unpolarized light: $I = I_0/2$.', 'p_polarization', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two Polaroids with their transmission axes perpendicular to each other transmit', 'No light (zero intensity)', 'Full intensity', 'Half intensity', 'Quarter intensity', 'option1', 'Crossed Polaroids: $I = I_0\cos^2 90° = 0$. No light passes through.', 'p_polarization', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Malus''s law states that the intensity of polarized light after passing through an analyser at angle $\theta$ is', '$I_0\sin\theta$', '$I_0\sin^2\theta$', '$I_0\cos\theta$', '$I_0\cos^2\theta$', 'option4', '$I = I_0\cos^2\theta$ where $\theta$ is the angle between the polarization direction and the analyser axis.', 'p_polarization', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Brewster''s angle for glass of refractive index $1.5$ is approximately', '$45°$', '$33.7°$', '$56.3°$', '$60°$', 'option3', '$\theta_B = \tan^{-1}(1.5) \approx 56.3°$.', 'p_polarization', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Polarized light of intensity $I_0$ passes through a Polaroid at $60°$ to the polarization direction. The transmitted intensity is', '$I_0/4$', '$I_0/2$', '$3I_0/4$', '$I_0\sqrt{3}/2$', 'option1', '$I = I_0\cos^2 60° = I_0 \times (1/2)^2 = I_0/4$.', 'p_polarization', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('At Brewster''s angle, the reflected and refracted rays are', 'Parallel', 'Perpendicular to each other', 'At $45°$', 'At $60°$', 'option2', 'At Brewster''s angle: $\theta_B + \theta_r = 90°$. The reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular.', 'p_polarization', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Light scattered by the atmosphere at $90°$ to the incident sunlight is', 'Partially polarized', 'Completely polarized', 'Unpolarized', 'Circularly polarized', 'option2', 'Rayleigh scattering at $90°$ produces completely polarized light (the scattered $\vec{E}$ is perpendicular to the scattering plane).', 'p_polarization', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Unpolarized light of intensity $I_0$ passes through two Polaroids with axes at $30°$. The final intensity is', '$I_0/4$', '$I_0/8$', '$3I_0/8$', '$I_0/2$', 'option3', 'After first Polaroid: $I_1 = I_0/2$. After second at $30°$: $I_2 = (I_0/2)\cos^2 30° = (I_0/2)(3/4) = 3I_0/8$.', 'p_polarization', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Circularly polarized light is produced when two perpendicular plane-polarized waves of equal amplitude have a phase difference of', '$2\pi$', '$\pi$', '$0$', '$\pi/2$', 'option4', 'Circular polarization: equal amplitudes, $90°$ ($\pi/2$) phase difference. If amplitudes differ, it is elliptical.', 'p_polarization', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Optical activity (rotation of plane of polarization) is exhibited by', 'Plain glass', 'Sugar solution and quartz', 'Water', 'Air', 'option2', 'Optically active substances (sugar, quartz, tartaric acid) rotate the plane of polarization of linearly polarized light.', 'p_polarization', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Three Polaroids $P_1$, $P_2$, $P_3$ have axes at $0°$, $45°$, $90°$ respectively. Unpolarized light of intensity $I_0$ passes through all three. The final intensity is', '$I_0/2$', '$I_0/4$', 'Zero', '$I_0/8$', 'option4', 'After $P_1$: $I_0/2$. After $P_2$ (at $45°$): $(I_0/2)\cos^2 45° = I_0/4$. After $P_3$ (at $45°$ to $P_2$): $(I_0/4)\cos^2 45° = I_0/8$.', 'p_polarization', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the middle Polaroid ($P_2$ at $45°$) is removed from the three-Polaroid setup above, the intensity becomes', '$I_0/8$', 'Zero', '$I_0/4$', '$I_0/2$', 'option2', 'Without $P_2$: $P_1$ and $P_3$ are crossed ($90°$ apart). $I = (I_0/2)\cos^2 90° = 0$.', 'p_polarization', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The specific rotation of a sugar solution is $66°$. A $20\;\text{cm}$ tube with $10\%$ concentration rotates the plane by', '$66°$', '$13.2°$', '$6.6°$', '$132°$', 'option2', 'Rotation $= [\alpha] \times l \times c = 66 \times 2 \times 0.1 = 13.2°$ (where $l$ is in dm and $c$ in g/mL).', 'p_polarization', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Elliptically polarized light can be decomposed into', 'Two perpendicular linearly polarized components with unequal amplitudes and a phase difference', 'Two parallel components', 'Three perpendicular components', 'A single component', 'option1', 'Elliptical polarization is the general case: two perpendicular components with arbitrary amplitude ratio and phase difference.', 'p_polarization', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A quarter-wave plate converts linearly polarized light (at $45°$ to the fast axis) into', 'Unpolarized light', 'Circularly polarized light', 'Elliptically polarized light always', 'Plane-polarized light rotated by $90°$', 'option2', 'A quarter-wave plate introduces $\pi/2$ phase difference. At $45°$ input, the two components are equal, producing circular polarization.', 'p_polarization', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The degree of polarization of partially polarized light is defined as', '$I_{min}/I_{max}$', '$I_{max}/I_{min}$', '$(I_{max} - I_{min})/(I_{max} + I_{min})$', '$(I_{max} + I_{min})/2$', 'option3', 'Degree of polarization $P = (I_{max} - I_{min})/(I_{max} + I_{min})$. $P = 0$ for unpolarized, $P = 1$ for fully polarized.', 'p_polarization', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A half-wave plate rotates the plane of polarization by', '$\theta$', '$2\theta$ where $\theta$ is the angle between the input polarization and the fast axis', '$90°$ always', '$180°$ always', 'option2', 'A half-wave plate introduces $\pi$ phase difference, effectively rotating the polarization direction by $2\theta$.', 'p_polarization', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_photoelectric_effect (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('The photoelectric effect was first observed by', 'Einstein', 'Hertz', 'Planck', 'Bohr', 'option2', 'Heinrich Hertz first observed the photoelectric effect in 1887. Einstein explained it theoretically in 1905.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the photoelectric effect, electrons are emitted when', 'Light of sufficiently high frequency falls on a metal surface', 'Any light falls on any surface', 'The metal is heated', 'An electric field is applied', 'option1', 'Photoelectric emission requires photon energy $h\nu \geq \phi$ (work function). Only sufficiently high frequency works.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The threshold frequency $\nu_0$ for a metal is the minimum frequency below which', 'No photoelectrons are emitted regardless of intensity', 'Maximum electrons are emitted', 'The current is maximum', 'The stopping potential is zero', 'option1', 'Below $\nu_0$: $h\nu < \phi$, so no electrons are emitted no matter how intense the light.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Increasing the intensity of light (above threshold frequency) in the photoelectric effect', 'Increases the maximum KE', 'Increases the number of photoelectrons but not their maximum KE', 'Decreases the number of electrons', 'Has no effect', 'option2', 'Higher intensity means more photons per second, hence more electrons. But each photon''s energy ($h\nu$) is unchanged, so max KE is unchanged.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The stopping potential in the photoelectric effect depends on', 'The frequency of incident light', 'The intensity of light', 'The area of the metal surface', 'The distance of the light source', 'option1', '$V_0 = (h\nu - \phi)/e$. Stopping potential depends on frequency, not intensity.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The work function of a metal is', 'The minimum energy needed to remove an electron from the metal surface', 'The maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons', 'The threshold wavelength', 'The stopping potential', 'option1', 'Work function $\phi = h\nu_0$ is the minimum energy to liberate an electron from the surface.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Lenard observed that the maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons', 'Increases with intensity', 'Is independent of the intensity of light', 'Decreases with frequency', 'Is always zero', 'option2', 'Lenard found that $KE_{max}$ depends on frequency, not intensity — a key observation that classical wave theory could not explain.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A metal has work function $4\;\text{eV}$. Light of energy $6\;\text{eV}$ falls on it. The maximum KE of photoelectrons is', '$10\;\text{eV}$', '$4\;\text{eV}$', '$2\;\text{eV}$', '$6\;\text{eV}$', 'option3', '$KE_{max} = h\nu - \phi = 6 - 4 = 2\;\text{eV}$.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The stopping potential for a metal with work function $2\;\text{eV}$ when illuminated by light of wavelength $400\;\text{nm}$ is approximately ($h = 6.63 \times 10^{-34}\;\text{J}\cdot\text{s}$)', '$1.1\;\text{V}$', '$2\;\text{V}$', '$3.1\;\text{V}$', '$0.5\;\text{V}$', 'option1', '$E = hc/\lambda = 6.63 \times 10^{-34} \times 3 \times 10^8/(400 \times 10^{-9}) = 4.97 \times 10^{-19}\;\text{J} = 3.1\;\text{eV}$. $V_0 = (3.1 - 2)/1 = 1.1\;\text{V}$.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The threshold wavelength for a metal with work function $\phi$ is', '$h\phi/c$', '$\phi/(hc)$', '$hc/\phi$', '$c/(h\phi)$', 'option3', '$\phi = hc/\lambda_0$. $\lambda_0 = hc/\phi$.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a photoelectric experiment, the graph of stopping potential $V_0$ vs frequency $\nu$ is', 'A horizontal line', 'A parabola', 'An exponential curve', 'A straight line with slope $h/e$', 'option4', '$V_0 = (h/e)\nu - \phi/e$. This is linear with slope $h/e$ and intercept $-\phi/e$.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The photoelectric effect is instantaneous (no time lag) because', 'Electrons are very light', 'The metal is a good conductor', 'Light travels very fast', 'Energy is transferred in discrete quanta (photons), not continuously', 'option4', 'A single photon delivers all its energy to one electron at once. No accumulation time is needed (unlike the wave model prediction).', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two metals $A$ and $B$ have work functions $2\;\text{eV}$ and $4\;\text{eV}$. For the same frequency of light, the ratio of maximum speeds of photoelectrons is', 'Cannot be determined without knowing the frequency', 'Always $2:1$', 'Always $1:2$', 'Always $\sqrt{2}:1$', 'option1', '$KE = h\nu - \phi$. Without knowing $\nu$, we cannot determine the ratio. It depends on the specific frequency used.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The photoelectric current is directly proportional to', 'The frequency of light', 'The intensity of incident light (above threshold)', 'The work function', 'The stopping potential', 'option2', 'More intensity means more photons per second, hence more photoelectrons per second, hence more current.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Light of wavelength $200\;\text{nm}$ falls on a metal of work function $4\;\text{eV}$. The maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons is', '$4\;\text{eV}$', '$6.2\;\text{eV}$', '$2.2\;\text{eV}$', '$10.2\;\text{eV}$', 'option3', '$E = hc/\lambda = 1240/200 = 6.2\;\text{eV}$. $KE_{max} = 6.2 - 4 = 2.2\;\text{eV}$.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The saturation current in a photoelectric experiment depends on', 'The intensity of incident light', 'The frequency of light', 'The work function', 'The stopping potential', 'option1', 'Saturation current $\propto$ number of photoelectrons/s $\propto$ intensity (for $\nu > \nu_0$).', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a photoelectric experiment, the slope of the $V_0$ vs $\nu$ graph gives', 'Planck''s constant divided by electron charge ($h/e$)', 'The work function', 'The threshold frequency', 'The electron mass', 'option1', '$V_0 = (h/e)\nu - \phi/e$. Slope $= h/e$. This is how Millikan measured $h$.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the frequency of incident light is doubled (keeping it above threshold), the stopping potential', 'Exactly doubles', 'More than doubles', 'Less than doubles', 'Remains the same', 'option2', '$V_0 = h\nu/e - \phi/e$. If $\nu \to 2\nu$: $V_0'' = 2h\nu/e - \phi/e = 2V_0 + \phi/e > 2V_0$. It more than doubles.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The photoelectric effect cannot be explained by the wave theory because the wave theory predicts', 'Frequency dependence', 'Instantaneous emission', 'Emission should depend on intensity (not frequency) and there should be a time lag', 'Discrete energy packets', 'option3', 'Wave theory predicts: (1) KE should depend on intensity, (2) emission at any frequency given enough time, (3) a time lag for energy accumulation. All contradicted by experiment.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two photons of energies $3\;\text{eV}$ and $5\;\text{eV}$ hit a metal of work function $4\;\text{eV}$ simultaneously. The maximum KE of the emitted electron is', '$4\;\text{eV}$', '$1\;\text{eV}$', '$8\;\text{eV}$', 'No emission occurs', 'option2', 'Each photon interacts with one electron independently. The $5\;\text{eV}$ photon can eject an electron with $KE = 5 - 4 = 1\;\text{eV}$. The $3\;\text{eV}$ photon cannot eject any electron. Multi-photon absorption is negligible at normal intensities.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The de Broglie wavelength of the fastest photoelectron emitted from a metal ($\phi = 2\;\text{eV}$) by light of energy $4\;\text{eV}$ is approximately', '$1.2\;\text{\AA}$', '$8.7\;\text{\AA}$', '$0.87\;\text{\AA}$', '$87\;\text{\AA}$', 'option2', '$KE = 4 - 2 = 2\;\text{eV} = 3.2 \times 10^{-19}\;\text{J}$. $\lambda = h/\sqrt{2mKE} = 6.63 \times 10^{-34}/\sqrt{2 \times 9.1 \times 10^{-31} \times 3.2 \times 10^{-19}} = 6.63 \times 10^{-34}/\sqrt{5.82 \times 10^{-49}} = 6.63 \times 10^{-34}/(7.63 \times 10^{-25}) = 8.69 \times 10^{-10}\;\text{m} = 8.7\;\text{\AA}$.', 'p_photoelectric_effect', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_einstein_equation (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('Einstein''s photoelectric equation is', '$KE_{max} = h\nu - \phi$', '$KE_{max} = h\nu + \phi$', '$KE_{max} = \phi - h\nu$', '$KE_{max} = h/\nu - \phi$', 'option1', '$KE_{max} = h\nu - \phi$ where $h\nu$ is the photon energy and $\phi$ is the work function.', 'p_einstein_equation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The energy of a photon of frequency $\nu$ is', '$h/\nu$', '$h\nu$', '$\nu/h$', '$h\nu^2$', 'option2', '$E = h\nu$ where $h = 6.63 \times 10^{-34}\;\text{J}\cdot\text{s}$ is Planck''s constant.', 'p_einstein_equation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The momentum of a photon of wavelength $\lambda$ is', '$h\lambda$', '$\lambda/h$', '$h/\lambda$', '$h/\lambda^2$', 'option3', '$p = h/\lambda = E/c$. Photons carry momentum despite having zero rest mass.', 'p_einstein_equation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Einstein received the Nobel Prize in Physics for', 'The theory of relativity', 'His explanation of the photoelectric effect', 'The discovery of X-rays', 'The Bohr model', 'option2', 'Einstein won the 1921 Nobel Prize for his explanation of the photoelectric effect using the photon concept.', 'p_einstein_equation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The particle nature of light is demonstrated by', 'Interference', 'Diffraction', 'The photoelectric effect and Compton scattering', 'Polarization', 'option3', 'Photoelectric effect and Compton scattering show light behaving as particles (photons). Interference and diffraction show wave nature.', 'p_einstein_equation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A photon of wavelength $\lambda$ has energy', '$hc\lambda$', '$h/(c\lambda)$', '$hc/\lambda$', '$\lambda/(hc)$', 'option3', '$E = h\nu = hc/\lambda$.', 'p_einstein_equation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The rest mass of a photon is', 'Equal to the electron mass', '$h\nu/c^2$', '$h/(c\lambda)$', 'Zero', 'option4', 'Photons have zero rest mass. Their relativistic mass-equivalent is $E/c^2 = h\nu/c^2$, but rest mass is zero.', 'p_einstein_equation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The energy of a photon of wavelength $500\;\text{nm}$ is', '$3.98 \times 10^{-19}\;\text{J}$', '$2.48\;\text{eV}$', '$3.98 \times 10^{-17}\;\text{J}$', 'Both option1 and option2', 'option4', '$E = hc/\lambda = 6.63 \times 10^{-34} \times 3 \times 10^8/(500 \times 10^{-9}) = 3.98 \times 10^{-19}\;\text{J} = 2.48\;\text{eV}$. Both are correct.', 'p_einstein_equation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The number of photons emitted per second by a source of power $P$ and wavelength $\lambda$ is', '$hcP/\lambda$', '$Phc/\lambda$', '$P/(h\lambda)$', '$P\lambda/(hc)$', 'option4', '$n = P/E = P/(hc/\lambda) = P\lambda/(hc)$.', 'p_einstein_equation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A $60\;\text{W}$ bulb emits light of wavelength $600\;\text{nm}$. The number of photons emitted per second is approximately', '$1.8 \times 10^{20}$', '$1.8 \times 10^{18}$', '$1.8 \times 10^{22}$', '$3.6 \times 10^{20}$', 'option1', '$n = P\lambda/(hc) = 60 \times 600 \times 10^{-9}/(6.63 \times 10^{-34} \times 3 \times 10^8) = 3.6 \times 10^{-5}/(1.989 \times 10^{-25}) = 1.81 \times 10^{20}$.', 'p_einstein_equation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ratio of momenta of two photons of wavelengths $\lambda_1$ and $\lambda_2$ is', '$\lambda_2/\lambda_1$', '$\lambda_1/\lambda_2$', '$\lambda_1^2/\lambda_2^2$', '$\sqrt{\lambda_2/\lambda_1}$', 'option1', '$p = h/\lambda$. $p_1/p_2 = \lambda_2/\lambda_1$.', 'p_einstein_equation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In Compton scattering, the change in wavelength of the photon depends on', 'Only the scattering angle', 'The initial wavelength', 'The material of the target', 'The intensity of the beam', 'option1', 'Compton shift: $\Delta\lambda = (h/(m_e c))(1 - \cos\theta)$. It depends only on the scattering angle $\theta$.', 'p_einstein_equation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Compton wavelength of the electron is', '$h/(m_e c) \approx 0.0243\;\text{\AA}$', '$m_e c/h$', '$h m_e c$', '$c/(m_e h)$', 'option1', '$\lambda_C = h/(m_e c) = 6.63 \times 10^{-34}/(9.1 \times 10^{-31} \times 3 \times 10^8) = 2.43 \times 10^{-12}\;\text{m} = 0.0243\;\text{\AA}$.', 'p_einstein_equation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The momentum of a photon of energy $3\;\text{eV}$ is approximately', '$1.6 \times 10^{-27}\;\text{kg}\cdot\text{m/s}$', '$1.6 \times 10^{-19}\;\text{kg}\cdot\text{m/s}$', '$4.8 \times 10^{-19}\;\text{kg}\cdot\text{m/s}$', '$3 \times 10^{-27}\;\text{kg}\cdot\text{m/s}$', 'option1', '$p = E/c = 3 \times 1.6 \times 10^{-19}/(3 \times 10^8) = 1.6 \times 10^{-27}\;\text{kg}\cdot\text{m/s}$.', 'p_einstein_equation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A photon of energy $E$ collides with a stationary electron. The maximum energy transferred to the electron occurs when the photon is', 'Scattered at $90°$', 'Forward scattered ($\theta = 0°$)', 'Backscattered ($\theta = 180°$)', 'Absorbed completely', 'option3', 'Maximum energy transfer in Compton scattering occurs at $\theta = 180°$ (head-on backscatter).', 'p_einstein_equation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The radiation pressure on a perfectly reflecting surface due to photons of intensity $I$ is', '$I/c^2$', '$I/c$', '$2I/c$', '$Ic$', 'option3', 'For perfect reflection, momentum change per photon $= 2p$. Pressure $= 2I/c$.', 'p_einstein_equation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A photon and an electron have the same energy $E$. The ratio of their wavelengths $\lambda_{photon}/\lambda_{electron}$ is', '$\sqrt{E/(2m_e c^2)}$', '$\sqrt{2m_e c^2/E}$', '$c\sqrt{2m_e/E}$', '$\sqrt{2m_e E}/h$', 'option3', '$\lambda_{ph} = hc/E$. $\lambda_e = h/\sqrt{2m_e E}$. Ratio $= (hc/E)/(h/\sqrt{2m_e E}) = c\sqrt{2m_e E}/E = c\sqrt{2m_e/E}$.', 'p_einstein_equation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A photon of wavelength $\lambda$ is scattered by a free electron at rest. After Compton scattering at $90°$, the wavelength of the scattered photon is', '$\lambda + h/(m_e c)$', '$\lambda - h/(m_e c)$', '$\lambda$', '$2\lambda$', 'option1', '$\lambda'' = \lambda + (h/(m_e c))(1 - \cos 90°) = \lambda + h/(m_e c)$.', 'p_einstein_equation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The equivalent mass of a photon of frequency $\nu$ is', '$h\nu c$', '$h\nu/c^2$', '$h/(c^2\nu)$', '$c^2/(h\nu)$', 'option2', 'From $E = mc^2$: $m = E/c^2 = h\nu/c^2$ (relativistic mass-equivalent, not rest mass).', 'p_einstein_equation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A laser emits $10^{18}$ photons per second at $\lambda = 630\;\text{nm}$. The power of the laser is approximately', '$0.32\;\text{W}$', '$3.2\;\text{W}$', '$0.032\;\text{W}$', '$32\;\text{W}$', 'option1', '$P = nhc/\lambda = 10^{18} \times 6.63 \times 10^{-34} \times 3 \times 10^8/(630 \times 10^{-9}) = 10^{18} \times 3.16 \times 10^{-19} = 0.316\;\text{W}$.', 'p_einstein_equation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The graph of photon energy vs wavelength is', 'A parabola', 'A straight line', 'A rectangular hyperbola ($E \propto 1/\lambda$)', 'An exponential', 'option3', '$E = hc/\lambda$. This is a rectangular hyperbola in the $E$-$\lambda$ plane.', 'p_einstein_equation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_matter_waves (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('The de Broglie wavelength of a particle of mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is', '$mv/h$', '$h/(mv)$', '$hm/v$', '$v/(hm)$', 'option2', '$\lambda = h/p = h/(mv)$. Every moving particle has an associated wavelength.', 'p_matter_waves', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The de Broglie hypothesis states that', 'Only photons have wave nature', 'All matter has an associated wave nature', 'Waves have particle nature only', 'Only electrons have wave nature', 'option2', 'de Broglie proposed that all matter (not just light) exhibits wave-particle duality: $\lambda = h/p$.', 'p_matter_waves', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The de Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated through potential $V$ is', '$heV/m$', '$\sqrt{2meV}/h$', '$h\sqrt{2meV}$', '$h/\sqrt{2meV}$', 'option4', '$KE = eV$. $p = \sqrt{2meV}$. $\lambda = h/p = h/\sqrt{2meV}$.', 'p_matter_waves', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The wave nature of electrons was experimentally confirmed by', 'Hertz', 'Davisson and Germer', 'Einstein', 'Bohr', 'option2', 'Davisson and Germer (1927) observed electron diffraction from a nickel crystal, confirming de Broglie''s hypothesis.', 'p_matter_waves', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the kinetic energy of a particle is doubled, its de Broglie wavelength', 'Doubles', 'Halves', 'Becomes $1/\sqrt{2}$ times', 'Becomes $\sqrt{2}$ times', 'option3', '$\lambda = h/\sqrt{2mKE}$. If $KE \to 2KE$: $\lambda'' = \lambda/\sqrt{2}$.', 'p_matter_waves', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A cricket ball of mass $0.15\;\text{kg}$ moving at $30\;\text{m/s}$ has de Broglie wavelength of order', '$10^{-34}\;\text{m}$', '$10^{-10}\;\text{m}$', '$10^{-20}\;\text{m}$', '$10^{-5}\;\text{m}$', 'option1', '$\lambda = h/(mv) = 6.63 \times 10^{-34}/(0.15 \times 30) = 6.63 \times 10^{-34}/4.5 \approx 1.5 \times 10^{-34}\;\text{m}$. Undetectably small.', 'p_matter_waves', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The de Broglie wavelength is significant (observable) for', 'Subatomic particles like electrons', 'Macroscopic objects like cars', 'Planets', 'All objects equally', 'option1', 'For macroscopic objects, $\lambda$ is negligibly small ($\sim 10^{-34}\;\text{m}$). Only for subatomic particles is $\lambda$ comparable to atomic dimensions.', 'p_matter_waves', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An electron is accelerated through $100\;\text{V}$. Its de Broglie wavelength is approximately', '$123\;\text{\AA}$', '$12.3\;\text{\AA}$', '$0.123\;\text{\AA}$', '$1.23\;\text{\AA}$', 'option4', '$\lambda = 12.27/\sqrt{V}\;\text{\AA} = 12.27/\sqrt{100} = 12.27/10 = 1.227\;\text{\AA}$.', 'p_matter_waves', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A proton and an electron have the same kinetic energy. The ratio $\lambda_p/\lambda_e$ is', '$\sqrt{m_p/m_e}$', '$\sqrt{m_e/m_p}$', '$m_e/m_p$', '$m_p/m_e$', 'option2', '$\lambda = h/\sqrt{2mKE}$. $\lambda_p/\lambda_e = \sqrt{m_e/m_p}$. Since $m_p \gg m_e$, $\lambda_p \ll \lambda_e$.', 'p_matter_waves', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A proton and an electron have the same de Broglie wavelength. Which has greater kinetic energy?', 'The proton', 'The electron', 'Both have the same KE', 'Cannot be determined', 'option2', 'Same $\lambda$ means same $p = h/\lambda$. $KE = p^2/(2m)$. Since $m_e < m_p$: $KE_e > KE_p$. The electron has greater KE.', 'p_matter_waves', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The de Broglie wavelength of a thermal neutron at temperature $T$ is', '$h/\sqrt{2mk_BT}$', '$h/\sqrt{3mk_BT}$', '$\sqrt{3mk_BT}/h$', '$k_BT/h$', 'option2', 'Average KE $= \frac{3}{2}k_BT$. $\lambda = h/\sqrt{2m \times \frac{3}{2}k_BT} = h/\sqrt{3mk_BT}$.', 'p_matter_waves', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An alpha particle and a proton are accelerated through the same potential difference. The ratio $\lambda_\alpha/\lambda_p$ is', '$2$', '$2\sqrt{2}$', '$1/2$', '$1/(2\sqrt{2})$', 'option4', '$\lambda = h/\sqrt{2mqV}$. $\lambda_\alpha/\lambda_p = \sqrt{m_p q_p/(m_\alpha q_\alpha)} = \sqrt{m_p \times e/(4m_p \times 2e)} = \sqrt{1/8} = 1/(2\sqrt{2})$.', 'p_matter_waves', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The de Broglie wavelength of an electron in the $n$th Bohr orbit of hydrogen is', '$2\pi r_n/n$', '$n \times 2\pi r_n$', '$r_n/n$', '$h/(m_e v_n)$', 'option4', 'Bohr''s quantization: $m_e v_n r_n = n\hbar$. $\lambda = h/(m_e v_n) = 2\pi r_n/n$. Both option1 and option4 are correct, but option4 is the direct de Broglie formula.', 'p_matter_waves', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The group velocity of matter waves is equal to', 'The speed of light', 'The phase velocity', 'The particle velocity', 'Zero', 'option3', 'Group velocity $v_g = d\omega/dk$ equals the particle velocity. Phase velocity $v_p = \omega/k = c^2/v > c$.', 'p_matter_waves', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An electron and a photon have the same wavelength $\lambda$. The ratio of their energies $E_e/E_{ph}$ is', '$h\lambda/(2m_e c^2)$', '$2m_e c\lambda/h$', '$\lambda/(2m_e c)$', '$h/(2m_e c\lambda)$', 'option4', '$E_e = p^2/(2m_e) = h^2/(2m_e\lambda^2)$. $E_{ph} = hc/\lambda$. Ratio $= h/(2m_e c\lambda)$.', 'p_matter_waves', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Heisenberg uncertainty principle states that', '$\Delta E \cdot \Delta t = 0$', '$\Delta x \cdot \Delta p = 0$', '$\Delta x \cdot \Delta p \leq \hbar/2$', '$\Delta x \cdot \Delta p \geq \hbar/2$', 'option4', 'The uncertainty principle: $\Delta x \cdot \Delta p \geq \hbar/2$. Position and momentum cannot both be precisely known simultaneously.', 'p_matter_waves', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An electron is confined to a box of width $1\;\text{\AA}$. The minimum uncertainty in its velocity is approximately', '$5.8 \times 10^{10}\;\text{m/s}$', '$5.8 \times 10^3\;\text{m/s}$', '$5.8 \times 10^7\;\text{m/s}$', '$5.8 \times 10^5\;\text{m/s}$', 'option4', '$\Delta p \geq \hbar/(2\Delta x) = 1.055 \times 10^{-34}/(2 \times 10^{-10}) = 5.275 \times 10^{-25}\;\text{kg}\cdot\text{m/s}$. $\Delta v = \Delta p/m_e = 5.275 \times 10^{-25}/(9.1 \times 10^{-31}) = 5.8 \times 10^5\;\text{m/s}$.', 'p_matter_waves', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The de Broglie wavelength of a relativistic particle with total energy $E$ and rest mass $m_0$ is', '$h/\sqrt{2m_0 E}$', '$hc/\sqrt{E^2 - (m_0 c^2)^2}$', '$hc/E$', '$h/(m_0 c)$', 'option2', 'Relativistic: $E^2 = (pc)^2 + (m_0 c^2)^2$. $p = \sqrt{E^2 - (m_0 c^2)^2}/c$. $\lambda = h/p = hc/\sqrt{E^2 - (m_0 c^2)^2}$.', 'p_matter_waves', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Davisson-Germer experiment, the electron beam was scattered by a nickel crystal. The diffraction condition used was', 'Snell''s law', 'Bragg''s law: $n\lambda = 2d\sin\theta$', 'Malus''s law', 'Brewster''s law', 'option2', 'The crystal acts as a diffraction grating. Bragg''s law $n\lambda = 2d\sin\theta$ was used to verify the de Broglie wavelength.', 'p_matter_waves', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The phase velocity of de Broglie waves for a non-relativistic particle is', 'Greater than $c$', 'Equal to $c$', 'Less than $c$', 'Equal to the particle velocity', 'option1', '$v_p = \omega/k = E/p = (p^2/(2m))/p \times (2\pi/\lambda)/(2\pi\nu)$... Actually $v_p = E/p$. For non-relativistic: $E = p^2/(2m) + m c^2 \approx mc^2$ (rest energy dominates). $v_p = E/p \approx mc^2/(mv) = c^2/v > c$. Phase velocity exceeds $c$ but carries no information.', 'p_matter_waves', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An electron microscope has better resolution than an optical microscope because', 'Electrons are charged', 'Electrons can have much shorter de Broglie wavelengths than visible light', 'Electrons move faster than light', 'Electron microscopes are larger', 'option2', 'Resolution $\propto 1/\lambda$. Accelerated electrons have $\lambda \sim 0.01\;\text{\AA}$, much shorter than visible light ($\sim 5000\;\text{\AA}$).', 'p_matter_waves', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_atomic_models (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('Rutherford''s alpha-particle scattering experiment led to the discovery of', 'The electron', 'The nucleus', 'The neutron', 'Radioactivity', 'option2', 'Most alpha particles passed through the gold foil, but a few were deflected at large angles, revealing a small, dense, positive nucleus.', 'p_atomic_models', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Bohr model, the angular momentum of an electron in the $n$th orbit is', '$nh$', '$n\hbar$', '$n^2\hbar$', '$\hbar/n$', 'option2', 'Bohr''s quantization: $L = mvr = n\hbar = nh/(2\pi)$.', 'p_atomic_models', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The radius of the $n$th Bohr orbit in hydrogen is proportional to', '$n$', '$n^2$', '$1/n$', '$1/n^2$', 'option2', '$r_n = a_0 n^2/Z$ where $a_0 = 0.529\;\text{\AA}$. For hydrogen ($Z = 1$): $r_n \propto n^2$.', 'p_atomic_models', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The energy of an electron in the $n$th Bohr orbit of hydrogen is', '$-13.6/n\;\text{eV}$', '$-13.6/n^2\;\text{eV}$', '$13.6/n^2\;\text{eV}$', '$-13.6n^2\;\text{eV}$', 'option2', '$E_n = -13.6/n^2\;\text{eV}$. Negative sign indicates bound state.', 'p_atomic_models', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Lyman series of hydrogen corresponds to transitions ending at', '$n = 1$', '$n = 2$', '$n = 3$', '$n = \infty$', 'option1', 'Lyman: to $n = 1$ (UV). Balmer: to $n = 2$ (visible). Paschen: to $n = 3$ (IR).', 'p_atomic_models', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ground state energy of hydrogen is', '$+13.6\;\text{eV}$', '$-13.6\;\text{eV}$', '$-3.4\;\text{eV}$', '$0\;\text{eV}$', 'option2', '$E_1 = -13.6/1^2 = -13.6\;\text{eV}$.', 'p_atomic_models', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Bohr radius (radius of the first orbit of hydrogen) is approximately', '$52.9\;\text{\AA}$', '$5.29\;\text{\AA}$', '$0.0529\;\text{\AA}$', '$0.529\;\text{\AA}$', 'option4', '$a_0 = \hbar^2/(m_e ke^2) = 0.529\;\text{\AA} = 0.529 \times 10^{-10}\;\text{m}$.', 'p_atomic_models', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The wavelength of the first line of the Balmer series ($n = 3 \to n = 2$) is approximately', '$410\;\text{nm}$', '$486\;\text{nm}$ (blue-green)', '$434\;\text{nm}$ (violet)', '$656\;\text{nm}$ (red)', 'option4', '$1/\lambda = R_H(1/4 - 1/9) = R_H(5/36)$. $\lambda = 36/(5R_H) = 36/(5 \times 1.097 \times 10^7) = 656\;\text{nm}$ (H-alpha, red).', 'p_atomic_models', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ionization energy of hydrogen from the ground state is', '$1.51\;\text{eV}$', '$3.4\;\text{eV}$', '$10.2\;\text{eV}$', '$13.6\;\text{eV}$', 'option4', 'Ionization energy $= |E_1| = 13.6\;\text{eV}$ (energy to remove electron from $n = 1$ to $n = \infty$).', 'p_atomic_models', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The energy required to excite hydrogen from $n = 1$ to $n = 2$ is', '$1.89\;\text{eV}$', '$13.6\;\text{eV}$', '$3.4\;\text{eV}$', '$10.2\;\text{eV}$', 'option4', '$\Delta E = E_2 - E_1 = -3.4 - (-13.6) = 10.2\;\text{eV}$.', 'p_atomic_models', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The speed of an electron in the $n$th Bohr orbit is proportional to', '$n$', '$1/n$', '$n^2$', '$1/n^2$', 'option2', '$v_n = e^2/(2\epsilon_0 nh) \propto 1/n$. Speed decreases with increasing orbit number.', 'p_atomic_models', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The number of spectral lines emitted when hydrogen atoms are excited to the $n$th level is', '$n-1$', '$n$', '$n(n-1)/2$', '$n^2$', 'option3', 'Total lines $= \binom{n}{2} = n(n-1)/2$ (all possible downward transitions).', 'p_atomic_models', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('For a hydrogen-like ion with atomic number $Z$, the energy of the $n$th level is', '$-13.6Z^2/n\;\text{eV}$', '$-13.6Z/n^2\;\text{eV}$', '$-13.6/(Zn^2)\;\text{eV}$', '$-13.6Z^2/n^2\;\text{eV}$', 'option4', '$E_n = -13.6Z^2/n^2\;\text{eV}$. For He$^+$ ($Z = 2$): $E_1 = -54.4\;\text{eV}$.', 'p_atomic_models', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The shortest wavelength in the Lyman series (series limit) is', '$364.8\;\text{nm}$', '$121.6\;\text{nm}$', '$91.2\;\text{nm}$', '$656\;\text{nm}$', 'option3', 'Series limit: $n \to \infty$ to $n = 1$. $1/\lambda = R_H(1 - 0) = R_H$. $\lambda = 1/R_H = 91.2\;\text{nm}$.', 'p_atomic_models', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Bohr model, the ratio of the orbital period of the electron in the $n$th orbit to the first orbit is', '$1/n^3$', '$n^2$', '$n$', '$n^3$', 'option4', '$T_n = 2\pi r_n/v_n \propto n^2/(1/n) = n^3$. $T_n/T_1 = n^3$.', 'p_atomic_models', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A hydrogen atom in the ground state absorbs a photon of energy $12.75\;\text{eV}$. The atom is excited to the level', '$n = 2$', '$n = 3$', '$n = 4$', '$n = 5$', 'option3', '$E_n = -13.6/n^2$. $E_1 + 12.75 = -13.6 + 12.75 = -0.85\;\text{eV}$. $-13.6/n^2 = -0.85$. $n^2 = 16$. $n = 4$.', 'p_atomic_models', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ratio of the longest wavelength in the Lyman series to the longest wavelength in the Balmer series is', '$27/5$', '$5/27$', '$3/4$', '$4/3$', 'option2', 'Longest Lyman: $n = 2 \to 1$: $1/\lambda_L = R_H(1 - 1/4) = 3R_H/4$. Longest Balmer: $n = 3 \to 2$: $1/\lambda_B = R_H(1/4 - 1/9) = 5R_H/36$. Ratio $= \lambda_L/\lambda_B = (5R_H/36)/(3R_H/4) = 5/27$.', 'p_atomic_models', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The magnetic moment of an electron in the $n$th Bohr orbit is', '$e\hbar/(2m_e)$', '$e\hbar/(2m_e n)$', '$n^2 e\hbar/(2m_e)$', '$ne\hbar/(2m_e)$', 'option4', '$\mu = IA = (e/(2\pi r/v))(\pi r^2) = evr/2 = e(n\hbar/m_e)/(2) = ne\hbar/(2m_e) = n\mu_B$ where $\mu_B$ is the Bohr magneton.', 'p_atomic_models', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A limitation of the Bohr model is that it', 'Cannot explain the spectra of multi-electron atoms or fine structure', 'Cannot predict the hydrogen spectrum', 'Violates energy conservation', 'Predicts wrong ionization energy for hydrogen', 'option1', 'Bohr''s model works only for one-electron systems. It cannot explain fine structure, Zeeman effect, or multi-electron spectra.', 'p_atomic_models', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The de Broglie explanation of Bohr''s quantization condition is that', 'An integral number of wavelengths must fit in the orbit circumference', 'The electron is a standing wave', 'The orbit must be circular', 'The wavelength must equal the orbit radius', 'option1', '$2\pi r_n = n\lambda$. This means $n$ complete de Broglie wavelengths fit around the orbit, forming a standing wave.', 'p_atomic_models', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Rydberg constant for hydrogen is approximately', '$1.097 \times 10^{-7}\;\text{m}^{-1}$', '$1.097 \times 10^7\;\text{m}^{-1}$', '$6.63 \times 10^{-34}\;\text{m}^{-1}$', '$9.1 \times 10^{-31}\;\text{m}^{-1}$', 'option2', '$R_H = m_e e^4/(8\epsilon_0^2 h^3 c) \approx 1.097 \times 10^7\;\text{m}^{-1}$.', 'p_atomic_models', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_nuclear_composition (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('The nucleus of an atom contains', 'Only electrons', 'Protons and neutrons', 'Only protons', 'Protons, neutrons, and electrons', 'option2', 'The nucleus contains protons (positive) and neutrons (neutral), collectively called nucleons.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The atomic mass unit (u) is defined as', 'The mass of a proton', '$1/12$ the mass of a $^{12}\text{C}$ atom', 'The mass of a neutron', 'The mass of an electron', 'option2', '$1\;\text{u} = 1.66054 \times 10^{-27}\;\text{kg} = 931.5\;\text{MeV}/c^2$.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Isotopes are atoms with the same', 'Mass number', 'Atomic number but different mass numbers', 'Number of neutrons', 'Number of electrons only', 'option2', 'Isotopes: same $Z$ (protons), different $A$ (mass number). Example: $^1\text{H}$, $^2\text{H}$, $^3\text{H}$.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The nuclear radius is approximately given by', '$R = R_0 A^{1/3}$ where $R_0 \approx 1.2\;\text{fm}$', '$R = R_0 A$', '$R = R_0 A^{2/3}$', '$R = R_0/A$', 'option1', '$R = R_0 A^{1/3}$ implies nuclear volume $\propto A$ (constant nuclear density).', 'p_nuclear_composition', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The mass defect of a nucleus is', 'The difference between the sum of individual nucleon masses and the actual nuclear mass', 'The mass of the nucleus', 'The mass of the electrons', 'Zero for all nuclei', 'option1', '$\Delta m = [Zm_p + (A-Z)m_n] - M_{nucleus}$. This mass is converted to binding energy.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Einstein''s mass-energy relation is', '$E = mc$', '$E = mc^2$', '$E = m/c^2$', '$E = m^2c$', 'option2', '$E = mc^2$. $1\;\text{u} = 931.5\;\text{MeV}/c^2$.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The nuclear density is approximately', '$2.3 \times 10^{17}\;\text{kg/m}^3$', '$2.3 \times 10^{10}\;\text{kg/m}^3$', '$10^3\;\text{kg/m}^3$', '$10^{30}\;\text{kg/m}^3$', 'option1', 'Nuclear density $\approx 2.3 \times 10^{17}\;\text{kg/m}^3$, independent of $A$ (constant for all nuclei).', 'p_nuclear_composition', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The mass defect of $^4_2\text{He}$ is $0.0304\;\text{u}$. Its binding energy is approximately', '$0.0304\;\text{MeV}$', '$28.3\;\text{MeV}$', '$2.83\;\text{MeV}$', '$283\;\text{MeV}$', 'option2', '$BE = \Delta m \times 931.5 = 0.0304 \times 931.5 = 28.3\;\text{MeV}$.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The number of neutrons in $^{56}_{26}\text{Fe}$ is', '$26$', '$56$', '$30$', '$82$', 'option3', '$N = A - Z = 56 - 26 = 30$ neutrons.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Isobars are atoms with the same', 'Atomic number', 'Mass number but different atomic numbers', 'Number of neutrons', 'Number of electrons', 'option2', 'Isobars: same $A$, different $Z$. Example: $^{40}_{18}\text{Ar}$ and $^{40}_{20}\text{Ca}$.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ratio of nuclear radius of $^{27}\text{Al}$ to $^{125}\text{Te}$ is', '$\sqrt[3]{27/125}$', '$5/3$', '$27/125$', '$3/5$', 'option4', '$R \propto A^{1/3}$. Ratio $= (27)^{1/3}/(125)^{1/3} = 3/5$.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The energy equivalent of $1\;\text{u}$ is', '$931.5\;\text{MeV}$', '$1.6 \times 10^{-19}\;\text{J}$', '$13.6\;\text{eV}$', '$0.511\;\text{MeV}$', 'option1', '$1\;\text{u} \times c^2 = 1.66054 \times 10^{-27} \times (3 \times 10^8)^2 = 1.4924 \times 10^{-10}\;\text{J} = 931.5\;\text{MeV}$.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Isotones are atoms with the same number of', 'Protons', 'Neutrons', 'Mass number', 'Electrons', 'option2', 'Isotones: same $N$ (neutrons), different $Z$. Example: $^{13}_6\text{C}$ and $^{14}_7\text{N}$ (both have $N = 7$).', 'p_nuclear_composition', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The nuclear force between two nucleons is', 'Repulsive at all distances', 'Only between protons', 'Only between neutrons', 'Charge-independent (same for p-p, n-n, p-n)', 'option4', 'The strong nuclear force is charge-independent and acts equally between all nucleon pairs.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The nuclear force is', 'Weak and long-range', 'Long-range like gravity', 'Electromagnetic in nature', 'Short-range (effective up to $\sim 2$-$3\;\text{fm}$) and strongly attractive', 'option4', 'The strong nuclear force has a range of $\sim 2$-$3\;\text{fm}$ and is much stronger than electromagnetic force at nuclear distances.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The mass of a proton is $1.00728\;\text{u}$ and neutron is $1.00866\;\text{u}$. The mass of $^4_2\text{He}$ is $4.00260\;\text{u}$. The binding energy per nucleon is approximately', '$7.07\;\text{MeV}$', '$28.3\;\text{MeV}$', '$14.1\;\text{MeV}$', '$3.54\;\text{MeV}$', 'option1', '$\Delta m = 2(1.00728) + 2(1.00866) - 4.00260 = 4.03188 - 4.00260 = 0.02928\;\text{u}$. $BE = 0.02928 \times 931.5 = 27.28\;\text{MeV}$. $BE/A = 27.28/4 = 6.82\;\text{MeV}$. Closest is $7.07\;\text{MeV}$ (using more precise masses).', 'p_nuclear_composition', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The nuclear force becomes repulsive at distances', 'At all distances', 'Greater than $3\;\text{fm}$', 'It is never repulsive', 'Less than about $0.5\;\text{fm}$', 'option4', 'At very short distances ($< 0.5\;\text{fm}$), the nuclear force has a repulsive core, preventing nucleons from overlapping.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The packing fraction of a nucleus is defined as', '$M/A$', '$(M - A)/A$ where $M$ is the atomic mass in u', '$A/M$', '$(A - M)/A$', 'option2', 'Packing fraction $= (M - A)/A$. Negative packing fraction indicates high stability (large binding energy per nucleon).', 'p_nuclear_composition', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The semi-empirical mass formula (Weizsäcker formula) includes terms for', 'Only volume energy', 'Volume, surface, Coulomb, asymmetry, and pairing energies', 'Only Coulomb energy', 'Volume and surface only', 'option2', 'The liquid drop model gives $BE = a_V A - a_S A^{2/3} - a_C Z(Z-1)/A^{1/3} - a_A(A-2Z)^2/A \pm \delta$.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Magic numbers in nuclear physics (2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82, 126) correspond to', 'Nuclei with completely filled nuclear shells (extra stability)', 'Unstable nuclei', 'Radioactive nuclei', 'Nuclei with maximum mass defect', 'option1', 'Nuclei with magic numbers of protons or neutrons are exceptionally stable, analogous to noble gas electron configurations.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The radius of a nucleus with $A = 64$ is approximately ($R_0 = 1.2\;\text{fm}$)', '$1.2\;\text{fm}$', '$76.8\;\text{fm}$', '$4.8\;\text{fm}$', '$19.2\;\text{fm}$', 'option3', '$R = R_0 A^{1/3} = 1.2 \times 64^{1/3} = 1.2 \times 4 = 4.8\;\text{fm}$.', 'p_nuclear_composition', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_binding_energy_fission_fusion (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('Binding energy of a nucleus is', 'The kinetic energy of nucleons', 'The energy of the nucleus', 'The mass of the nucleus', 'The energy needed to separate all nucleons to infinity', 'option4', '$BE = \Delta m \times c^2$. It is the energy required to completely disassemble the nucleus into free nucleons.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The binding energy per nucleon is maximum (most stable) for nuclei near', '$A \approx 1$ (hydrogen)', '$A \approx 4$ (helium)', '$A \approx 238$ (uranium)', '$A \approx 56$ (iron)', 'option4', 'The BE/A curve peaks around $A = 56$ ($^{56}\text{Fe}$) at about $8.8\;\text{MeV}$/nucleon.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Nuclear fission is the process of', 'A nucleus emitting an alpha particle', 'Two light nuclei combining', 'A heavy nucleus splitting into two lighter nuclei', 'A nucleus absorbing a neutron without splitting', 'option3', 'Fission: a heavy nucleus (like $^{235}\text{U}$) splits into two medium-mass fragments, releasing energy and neutrons.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Nuclear fusion is the process of', 'A heavy nucleus splitting', 'Two light nuclei combining to form a heavier nucleus', 'Radioactive decay', 'Neutron capture', 'option2', 'Fusion: light nuclei (like hydrogen isotopes) combine to form heavier nuclei, releasing enormous energy. Powers the Sun.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Energy is released in fission because the products have', 'Lower binding energy per nucleon', 'Higher binding energy per nucleon than the parent', 'The same binding energy', 'Zero binding energy', 'option2', 'Fission products (medium $A$) have higher BE/A than the heavy parent, so energy is released.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Energy is released in fusion because the product has', 'Lower binding energy per nucleon', 'Higher binding energy per nucleon than the reactants', 'The same binding energy', 'Negative binding energy', 'option2', 'Fusion products have higher BE/A than the light reactants, releasing energy.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A chain reaction in nuclear fission is sustained when', 'All neutrons escape', 'No neutrons are produced', 'At least one neutron from each fission causes another fission', 'The temperature is very low', 'option3', 'For a sustained chain reaction, the multiplication factor $k \geq 1$ (at least one neutron per fission triggers another).', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The energy released per fission of $^{235}\text{U}$ is approximately', '$200\;\text{MeV}$', '$2\;\text{MeV}$', '$20\;\text{MeV}$', '$2000\;\text{MeV}$', 'option1', 'Each $^{235}\text{U}$ fission releases $\sim 200\;\text{MeV}$, mostly as kinetic energy of fission fragments.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a nuclear reactor, the moderator is used to', 'Slow down neutrons', 'Speed up neutrons', 'Absorb all neutrons', 'Produce neutrons', 'option1', 'The moderator (heavy water, graphite) slows fast neutrons to thermal energies, increasing the probability of fission.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Control rods in a nuclear reactor are made of', 'Lead', 'Uranium', 'Cadmium or boron (neutron absorbers)', 'Graphite', 'option3', 'Control rods absorb excess neutrons to regulate the chain reaction rate.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The fusion reaction in the Sun primarily converts', 'Hydrogen to helium', 'Helium to carbon', 'Uranium to lead', 'Carbon to oxygen', 'option1', 'The proton-proton chain: $4^1\text{H} \to ^4\text{He} + 2e^+ + 2\nu_e + 26.7\;\text{MeV}$.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Fusion requires extremely high temperatures because', 'Neutrons must be produced first', 'Nuclei are very heavy', 'The Coulomb repulsion between nuclei must be overcome', 'Electrons must be removed', 'option3', 'At $\sim 10^7$-$10^8\;\text{K}$, nuclei have enough kinetic energy to overcome Coulomb repulsion and get close enough for the strong force to act.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The critical mass of a fissile material is', 'The minimum mass needed to sustain a chain reaction', 'The maximum mass before explosion', 'The mass of one atom', 'The mass of the moderator', 'option1', 'Below critical mass, too many neutrons escape without causing fission. At critical mass, $k = 1$.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Q-value of a nuclear reaction is', 'The number of neutrons', 'The charge of the nucleus', 'The energy released or absorbed ($Q = (m_{reactants} - m_{products})c^2$)', 'The binding energy', 'option3', '$Q > 0$: exothermic (energy released). $Q < 0$: endothermic (energy absorbed).', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the reaction $^2_1\text{H} + ^3_1\text{H} \to ^4_2\text{He} + ^1_0\text{n}$, the energy released is $17.6\;\text{MeV}$. The energy per nucleon of the reactants converted to energy is', '$4.4\;\text{MeV}$', '$17.6\;\text{MeV}$', '$3.52\;\text{MeV}$', '$8.8\;\text{MeV}$', 'option3', 'Total nucleons in reactants $= 5$. Energy per nucleon $= 17.6/5 = 3.52\;\text{MeV}$.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If $1\;\text{kg}$ of $^{235}\text{U}$ undergoes complete fission ($200\;\text{MeV}$ per fission), the energy released is approximately', '$8.2 \times 10^{16}\;\text{J}$', '$8.2 \times 10^{10}\;\text{J}$', '$8.2 \times 10^{13}\;\text{J}$', '$2 \times 10^{11}\;\text{J}$', 'option3', 'Number of atoms $= 1000/(235 \times 1.66 \times 10^{-27}) = 2.56 \times 10^{24}$. Energy $= 2.56 \times 10^{24} \times 200 \times 10^6 \times 1.6 \times 10^{-19} = 8.2 \times 10^{13}\;\text{J}$.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The binding energy per nucleon of $^{56}\text{Fe}$ is about $8.8\;\text{MeV}$ and of $^{235}\text{U}$ is about $7.6\;\text{MeV}$. The energy released per nucleon in fission of $^{235}\text{U}$ (assuming products near Fe) is approximately', '$1.2\;\text{MeV}$', '$8.8\;\text{MeV}$', '$7.6\;\text{MeV}$', '$0.9\;\text{MeV}$', 'option4', 'Energy per nucleon $\approx 8.8 - 7.6 = 1.2\;\text{MeV}$. Total $\approx 1.2 \times 235 \approx 282\;\text{MeV}$. But actual fission gives $\sim 200\;\text{MeV}$ because products are not exactly Fe. Per nucleon: $200/235 \approx 0.85\;\text{MeV}$. Closest is $0.9\;\text{MeV}$.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A nuclear reactor produces $1000\;\text{MW}$ of power. The mass of $^{235}\text{U}$ consumed per day is approximately ($200\;\text{MeV}$ per fission)', '$10.5\;\text{kg}$', '$1.05\;\text{kg}$', '$105\;\text{kg}$', '$0.105\;\text{kg}$', 'option2', 'Energy/day $= 10^9 \times 86400 = 8.64 \times 10^{13}\;\text{J}$. Fissions/day $= 8.64 \times 10^{13}/(200 \times 10^6 \times 1.6 \times 10^{-19}) = 2.7 \times 10^{24}$. Mass $= 2.7 \times 10^{24} \times 235 \times 1.66 \times 10^{-27} = 1.05\;\text{kg}$.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Thermonuclear fusion on Earth (as in a hydrogen bomb or tokamak) requires', 'Control rods', 'Room temperature', 'A moderator', 'Temperatures of $\sim 10^8\;\text{K}$ and plasma confinement', 'option4', 'Controlled fusion requires extreme temperatures for plasma and magnetic/inertial confinement to sustain the reaction.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The stellar nucleosynthesis of elements heavier than iron primarily occurs during', 'Supernova explosions', 'Main sequence hydrogen burning', 'Red giant helium burning', 'White dwarf cooling', 'option1', 'Elements heavier than Fe require energy input (endothermic fusion). They are primarily formed in supernovae via rapid neutron capture (r-process).', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The mass of the Sun decreases by approximately $4 \times 10^9\;\text{kg}$ every second due to fusion. The power output of the Sun is approximately', '$3.6 \times 10^{20}\;\text{W}$', '$4 \times 10^9\;\text{W}$', '$3.6 \times 10^{26}\;\text{W}$', '$9 \times 10^{16}\;\text{W}$', 'option3', '$P = \Delta m \times c^2/t = 4 \times 10^9 \times (3 \times 10^8)^2/1 = 3.6 \times 10^{26}\;\text{W}$.', 'p_binding_energy_fission_fusion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_semiconductor_diode (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('In an intrinsic semiconductor, the number of free electrons equals', 'Twice the number of holes', 'The number of holes', 'Zero', 'The number of atoms', 'option2', 'In a pure semiconductor, every electron that jumps to the conduction band leaves a hole. $n_e = n_h = n_i$.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An n-type semiconductor is formed by doping silicon with', 'A trivalent element like boron', 'A pentavalent element like phosphorus', 'A tetravalent element', 'A metal', 'option2', 'Pentavalent dopants (P, As, Sb) provide extra electrons, making it n-type.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A p-type semiconductor is formed by doping silicon with', 'A pentavalent element', 'A trivalent element like boron', 'A monovalent element', 'A noble gas', 'option2', 'Trivalent dopants (B, Al, Ga) create holes (missing electrons), making it p-type.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a p-n junction diode, the depletion region is', 'Rich in mobile charge carriers', 'Devoid of mobile charge carriers', 'Present only in the p-side', 'Present only in the n-side', 'option2', 'The depletion region has immobile ions but no free electrons or holes — carriers have diffused away.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A p-n junction diode in forward bias has', 'n-side connected to positive terminal', 'p-side connected to positive terminal of battery', 'No current flow', 'Infinite resistance', 'option2', 'Forward bias: p to positive, n to negative. This reduces the barrier and allows current flow.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The barrier potential of a silicon p-n junction is approximately', '$0.3\;\text{V}$', '$0.7\;\text{V}$', '$1.1\;\text{V}$', '$0\;\text{V}$', 'option2', 'Silicon: $\sim 0.7\;\text{V}$. Germanium: $\sim 0.3\;\text{V}$.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A half-wave rectifier converts', 'DC to AC', 'AC to pulsating DC (using one half of each cycle)', 'AC to smooth DC', 'DC to pulsating DC', 'option2', 'A half-wave rectifier uses one diode to pass only one half of the AC cycle.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In reverse bias, the current through a p-n junction is', 'Very small (reverse saturation current)', 'Very large', 'Zero exactly', 'Equal to forward current', 'option1', 'Reverse current is due to minority carriers and is very small ($\sim \mu\text{A}$ for Si).', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The I-V characteristic of a p-n junction diode shows', 'Linear relationship', 'Exponential increase in forward bias, small constant current in reverse', 'Constant current in both directions', 'No current in either direction', 'option2', '$I = I_0(e^{eV/(k_BT)} - 1)$. Exponential in forward, $\approx -I_0$ in reverse.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A full-wave rectifier uses', 'Two diodes (centre-tap) or four diodes (bridge)', 'One diode', 'No diodes', 'A capacitor only', 'option1', 'Full-wave: centre-tap transformer + 2 diodes, or bridge rectifier with 4 diodes.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ripple factor of a half-wave rectifier is', '$2.0$', '$0.48$', '$0$', '$1.21$', 'option4', 'Ripple factor $= I_{ac}/I_{dc} = 1.21$ for half-wave, $0.48$ for full-wave.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The majority carriers in an n-type semiconductor are', 'Holes', 'Electrons', 'Protons', 'Neutrons', 'option2', 'In n-type: majority carriers are electrons (from donor atoms), minority carriers are holes.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The conductivity of a semiconductor increases with temperature because', 'More electron-hole pairs are generated', 'Resistance increases', 'Fewer carriers are available', 'The band gap increases', 'option1', 'Higher temperature provides energy to excite more electrons across the band gap, increasing carrier concentration.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The energy band gap of silicon is approximately', '$0.67\;\text{eV}$', '$1.1\;\text{eV}$', '$5.5\;\text{eV}$', '$0.1\;\text{eV}$', 'option2', 'Si: $1.1\;\text{eV}$. Ge: $0.67\;\text{eV}$. Diamond: $5.5\;\text{eV}$ (insulator).', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Reverse breakdown in a p-n junction can occur by', 'Magnetic field', 'Forward bias only', 'Heating', 'Zener effect (tunnelling) or avalanche effect', 'option4', 'Zener breakdown: tunnelling at high fields (narrow depletion). Avalanche: impact ionization at high reverse voltage.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The dynamic resistance of a diode in forward bias is', '$dV/dI$ (slope of V-I curve)', '$V/I$', 'Constant', 'Infinite', 'option1', 'Dynamic (AC) resistance $r_d = dV/dI$, which is small in forward bias ($\sim$ few ohms).', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a bridge rectifier, during each half cycle, the number of diodes conducting is', '$2$', '$4$', '$1$', '$3$', 'option1', 'In each half cycle, two diodes conduct (in series) while the other two are reverse-biased.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The diffusion current in a p-n junction is due to', 'Electric field in the depletion region', 'Concentration gradient of charge carriers', 'External voltage', 'Magnetic field', 'option2', 'Diffusion current flows because of the concentration difference of electrons and holes across the junction.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The drift current in a p-n junction is due to', 'The electric field in the depletion region acting on minority carriers', 'Concentration gradient', 'External voltage only', 'Temperature gradient', 'option1', 'The built-in electric field sweeps minority carriers across the junction, creating drift current.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('At thermal equilibrium in a p-n junction (no external bias), the net current is', 'Zero (diffusion current = drift current)', 'Maximum', 'Equal to the saturation current', 'Infinite', 'option1', 'At equilibrium, diffusion and drift currents balance exactly, giving zero net current.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A capacitor filter is used after a rectifier to', 'Smooth the pulsating DC output', 'Increase the voltage', 'Decrease the current', 'Convert DC to AC', 'option1', 'The capacitor charges during peaks and discharges during troughs, reducing ripple in the output.', 'p_semiconductor_diode', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_led_photodiode_zener (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('A Zener diode is used as', 'A switch', 'An amplifier', 'A current source', 'A voltage regulator', 'option4', 'In reverse breakdown, a Zener diode maintains a nearly constant voltage across it, making it ideal for voltage regulation.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An LED emits light when', 'Electrons and holes recombine at the junction in forward bias', 'It is reverse biased', 'Current flows through a resistor', 'It is heated', 'option1', 'In forward bias, electron-hole recombination releases energy as photons (if the band gap is suitable).', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A photodiode is operated in', 'Reverse bias', 'Forward bias', 'No bias', 'Alternating bias', 'option1', 'In reverse bias, the photocurrent is proportional to light intensity. The depletion region is wide, maximizing photon absorption.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A solar cell converts', 'Sound to electricity', 'Electrical energy to light', 'Heat to electricity', 'Light energy to electrical energy', 'option4', 'A solar cell (photovoltaic cell) uses the photovoltaic effect to generate voltage and current from sunlight.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Zener diode is connected in a circuit in', 'Reverse bias for voltage regulation', 'Forward bias for regulation', 'Series with the load', 'Parallel with the source', 'option1', 'The Zener is reverse-biased and connected in parallel with the load to maintain constant voltage.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('LEDs are made from', 'Compound semiconductors like GaAs, GaP', 'Pure silicon', 'Germanium', 'Metals', 'option1', 'LEDs use direct band gap semiconductors (GaAs, GaP, GaN) for efficient photon emission.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The colour of light emitted by an LED depends on', 'The size of the LED', 'The current through it', 'The voltage applied', 'The band gap of the semiconductor material', 'option4', '$E_{photon} = E_g$ (band gap). Different materials give different colours: GaAs (IR), GaP (green/red), GaN (blue).', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A Zener diode with breakdown voltage $5.6\;\text{V}$ is used with a series resistor of $200\;\Omega$ and input voltage $12\;\text{V}$. The current through the resistor is', '$60\;\text{mA}$', '$32\;\text{mA}$', '$28\;\text{mA}$', '$56\;\text{mA}$', 'option2', '$I = (V_{in} - V_Z)/R = (12 - 5.6)/200 = 6.4/200 = 0.032\;\text{A} = 32\;\text{mA}$.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The photocurrent in a photodiode is proportional to', 'The intensity of incident light', 'The square of intensity', 'The wavelength', 'The reverse voltage', 'option1', 'Each photon generates one electron-hole pair. More photons (higher intensity) means more photocurrent.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A solar cell operates without any external bias because', 'It uses thermal energy', 'It has a battery inside', 'The built-in potential of the p-n junction separates photo-generated carriers', 'It stores charge', 'option3', 'Photo-generated electron-hole pairs are separated by the junction''s built-in electric field, creating a voltage.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The open-circuit voltage of a typical silicon solar cell is approximately', '$1.5\;\text{V}$', '$0.5$-$0.6\;\text{V}$', '$12\;\text{V}$', '$0.1\;\text{V}$', 'option2', 'A single Si solar cell produces $\sim 0.5$-$0.6\;\text{V}$ open circuit. Multiple cells are connected in series for higher voltage.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a Zener regulator, if the input voltage increases, the Zener current', 'Becomes zero', 'Decreases', 'Remains the same', 'Increases (absorbing the extra current)', 'option4', 'The series resistor drops more voltage, increasing total current. Load current stays constant, so Zener current increases.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The forward voltage drop of a typical red LED is approximately', '$0.7\;\text{V}$', '$1.8$-$2.0\;\text{V}$', '$3.0$-$3.5\;\text{V}$', '$5\;\text{V}$', 'option2', 'Red LED: $\sim 1.8$-$2.0\;\text{V}$. Blue/white LED: $\sim 3.0$-$3.5\;\text{V}$. Si diode: $\sim 0.7\;\text{V}$.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The response time of a photodiode is typically', 'Minutes', 'Seconds', 'Nanoseconds (very fast)', 'Milliseconds', 'option3', 'Photodiodes have very fast response ($\sim$ ns) due to the thin depletion region, making them suitable for optical communication.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The efficiency of a solar cell is limited by', 'No theoretical limit', 'The Shockley-Queisser limit ($\sim 33\%$ for single junction)', 'Only manufacturing defects', 'The size of the cell', 'option2', 'The theoretical maximum efficiency for a single-junction solar cell is $\sim 33\%$ due to thermodynamic and spectral mismatch losses.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A Zener diode has $V_Z = 6\;\text{V}$ and maximum power rating $0.5\;\text{W}$. The maximum Zener current is', '$83.3\;\text{mA}$', '$3\;\text{A}$', '$0.5\;\text{A}$', '$12\;\text{mA}$', 'option1', '$I_{max} = P/V_Z = 0.5/6 = 0.0833\;\text{A} = 83.3\;\text{mA}$.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An avalanche photodiode (APD) differs from a regular photodiode in that', 'It only detects infrared', 'It operates in forward bias', 'It has no p-n junction', 'It has internal gain due to impact ionization', 'option4', 'APDs operate at high reverse bias where photo-generated carriers gain enough energy to create additional pairs (avalanche multiplication).', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The quantum efficiency of a photodiode is defined as', 'Voltage per unit current', 'Power output per power input', 'Current per unit voltage', 'Number of electron-hole pairs generated per incident photon', 'option4', 'Quantum efficiency $\eta = $ (number of carriers collected)/(number of incident photons). Ideally $\eta \leq 1$ for standard photodiodes.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The temperature coefficient of a Zener diode with $V_Z > 6\;\text{V}$ is typically', 'Positive (voltage increases with temperature)', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'Unpredictable', 'option1', 'For $V_Z > 6\;\text{V}$: avalanche breakdown dominates, giving positive temperature coefficient. For $V_Z < 4\;\text{V}$: Zener (tunnelling) gives negative coefficient.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A solar panel of $20\%$ efficiency and area $2\;\text{m}^2$ receives solar radiation of $1000\;\text{W/m}^2$. The electrical power output is', '$200\;\text{W}$', '$400\;\text{W}$', '$2000\;\text{W}$', '$100\;\text{W}$', 'option2', '$P = \eta \times I \times A = 0.20 \times 1000 \times 2 = 400\;\text{W}$.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An LED emitting at $620\;\text{nm}$ has a band gap of approximately', '$3.0\;\text{eV}$', '$1.1\;\text{eV}$', '$2.0\;\text{eV}$', '$0.7\;\text{eV}$', 'option3', '$E_g = hc/\lambda = 1240/620 = 2.0\;\text{eV}$.', 'p_led_photodiode_zener', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

