-- ============================================================
('Surface tension of a liquid can be measured by', 'A galvanometer', 'A thermometer', 'Capillary rise method', 'A spectrometer', 'option3', 'Surface tension $\gamma = r\rho g h/(2\cos\theta)$ from the height of liquid rise in a capillary tube.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The coefficient of viscosity can be determined using', 'A calorimeter', 'Stoke''s law (falling sphere method)', 'A Wheatstone bridge', 'A prism', 'option2', 'A sphere falling through a viscous liquid reaches terminal velocity: $\eta = 2r^2(\rho_s - \rho_l)g/(9v_t)$.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The speed of sound in air can be measured using', 'A resonance tube', 'A metre bridge', 'A potentiometer', 'A screw gauge', 'option1', 'A resonance tube uses standing waves in an air column to find the wavelength, then $v = f\lambda$.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Specific heat capacity of a liquid is measured using', 'A Vernier caliper', 'A calorimeter (method of mixtures)', 'A galvanometer', 'A telescope', 'option2', 'Method of mixtures: heat lost by hot body = heat gained by cold liquid. $m_1 c_1 \Delta T_1 = m_2 c_2 \Delta T_2$.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the capillary rise method, the liquid rises higher when the capillary tube is', 'Wider', 'Narrower', 'Longer', 'Made of metal', 'option2', '$h = 2\gamma\cos\theta/(\rho g r)$. Smaller $r$ gives larger $h$.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a resonance tube experiment, the first resonance occurs at length $l_1$ and the second at $l_2$. The speed of sound is', '$4f(l_2 - l_1)$', '$f(l_2 - l_1)$', '$2f(l_2 - l_1)$', '$f(l_2 + l_1)$', 'option3', '$l_1 + e = \lambda/4$, $l_2 + e = 3\lambda/4$. $l_2 - l_1 = \lambda/2$. $v = f\lambda = 2f(l_2 - l_1)$.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The end correction in a resonance tube is', '$e = l_1$', '$e = (l_2 - 3l_1)/2$', '$e = l_2 - l_1$', '$e = (l_2 + l_1)/2$', 'option2', 'From $l_1 + e = \lambda/4$ and $l_2 + e = 3\lambda/4$: $e = (l_2 - 3l_1)/2$.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a resonance tube, the first resonance is at $16\;\text{cm}$ and the second at $50\;\text{cm}$. The wavelength is', '$17\;\text{cm}$', '$34\;\text{cm}$', '$66\;\text{cm}$', '$68\;\text{cm}$', 'option4', '$\lambda = 2(l_2 - l_1) = 2(50 - 16) = 68\;\text{cm}$.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the falling sphere method for viscosity, the sphere must reach', 'Acceleration', 'Maximum velocity', 'Zero velocity', 'Terminal velocity before timing begins', 'option4', 'At terminal velocity, the net force is zero and the sphere falls at constant speed.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a calorimetry experiment, the water equivalent of the calorimeter is', 'The mass of water that would absorb the same heat as the calorimeter for the same temperature rise', 'The mass of the calorimeter', 'The volume of water in the calorimeter', 'The specific heat of the calorimeter', 'option1', 'Water equivalent $W = m_c c_c/c_w$ where $c_w$ is the specific heat of water.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the capillary rise experiment, the meniscus should be', 'Convex for water', 'Concave for water (wetting liquid)', 'Flat', 'Absent', 'option2', 'Water wets glass ($\theta < 90°$), forming a concave meniscus. Mercury ($\theta > 90°$) forms a convex meniscus.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The end correction in a resonance tube accounts for', 'The weight of the air column', 'Temperature effects', 'Humidity', 'The antinode being slightly above the open end of the tube', 'option4', 'The effective length of the air column extends slightly beyond the open end ($e \approx 0.6r$ where $r$ is the tube radius).', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a calorimetry experiment, radiation losses are minimized by', 'Using a black calorimeter', 'Using a polished calorimeter with a lid and stirring', 'Leaving the calorimeter open', 'Using a very large calorimeter', 'option2', 'Polished surfaces reduce radiation. A lid reduces convection. Stirring ensures uniform temperature.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Newton''s law of cooling can be verified experimentally by plotting', '$\ln(T - T_0)$ vs $t$ (should be linear)', '$T$ vs $t$', '$T^2$ vs $t$', '$1/T$ vs $t$', 'option1', '$dT/dt = -k(T - T_0)$. Solution: $\ln(T - T_0) = -kt + C$. A plot of $\ln(T - T_0)$ vs $t$ is linear with slope $-k$.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a resonance tube with a tuning fork of $512\;\text{Hz}$, the first resonance is at $16\;\text{cm}$. The speed of sound is approximately ($e = 1\;\text{cm}$)', '$256\;\text{m/s}$', '$328\;\text{m/s}$', '$512\;\text{m/s}$', '$348\;\text{m/s}$', 'option4', '$\lambda/4 = l_1 + e = 17\;\text{cm}$. $\lambda = 68\;\text{cm}$. $v = f\lambda = 512 \times 0.68 = 348\;\text{m/s}$.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Stoke''s law experiment, if the radius of the sphere is doubled (same material), the terminal velocity becomes', '$2$ times', '$4$ times', '$8$ times', '$16$ times', 'option2', '$v_t \propto r^2$. If $r \to 2r$: $v_t \to 4v_t$.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The surface tension of water at room temperature is approximately', '$0.73\;\text{N/m}$', '$0.073\;\text{N/m}$', '$7.3\;\text{N/m}$', '$0.0073\;\text{N/m}$', 'option2', 'Surface tension of water at $20°\text{C}$ is about $0.073\;\text{N/m}$ ($73\;\text{dyn/cm}$).', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a capillary tube of radius $0.5\;\text{mm}$, water ($\gamma = 0.073\;\text{N/m}$, $\rho = 1000\;\text{kg/m}^3$) rises to a height of approximately', '$5.94\;\text{cm}$', '$1.49\;\text{cm}$', '$2.97\;\text{cm}$', '$0.73\;\text{cm}$', 'option3', '$h = 2\gamma/(\rho g r) = 2 \times 0.073/(1000 \times 9.8 \times 0.5 \times 10^{-3}) = 0.146/4.9 = 0.0298\;\text{m} = 2.98\;\text{cm}$.', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a resonance tube, the ratio of lengths for the first and second resonance is approximately', '$1:3$', '$1:2$', '$1:4$', '$2:3$', 'option1', '$l_1 \approx \lambda/4$, $l_2 \approx 3\lambda/4$. Ratio $\approx 1:3$ (ignoring end correction).', 'p_exp_fluids_heat', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

