('A metre bridge is used to measure', 'Frequency', 'Capacitance', 'Inductance', 'Unknown resistance', 'option4', 'A metre bridge (slide wire bridge) works on the Wheatstone bridge principle to measure unknown resistance.', 'p_exp_electricity', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a metre bridge experiment, the balance point should ideally be near', 'The middle of the wire', 'One end', 'Either end', 'It does not matter', 'option1', 'Near the middle gives maximum sensitivity and minimum percentage error in the balance length.', 'p_exp_electricity', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ohm''s law can be verified using', 'A calorimeter', 'Only a galvanometer', 'A spectrometer', 'A voltmeter, ammeter, rheostat, and battery', 'option4', 'Plot $V$ vs $I$ for a conductor. A straight line through the origin confirms Ohm''s law.', 'p_exp_electricity', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The figure of merit of a galvanometer is', 'The maximum current it can measure', 'The current required for one division deflection', 'Its resistance', 'Its sensitivity', 'option2', 'Figure of merit $= I_g/N$ where $N$ is the number of divisions for full-scale deflection.', 'p_exp_electricity', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Resistivity of a wire can be measured using', 'A metre bridge and a screw gauge', 'A calorimeter', 'A spectrometer', 'A telescope', 'option1', 'Measure $R$ using metre bridge, then $\rho = RA/L$ where $A$ is measured with screw gauge and $L$ with metre scale.', 'p_exp_electricity', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a potentiometer experiment, the wire must have', 'Zero resistance', 'Variable cross-section', 'Uniform cross-section and resistivity', 'Very high resistance', 'option3', 'Uniform wire ensures the potential drop is proportional to length, which is essential for the potentiometer principle.', 'p_exp_electricity', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The sensitivity of a potentiometer can be increased by', 'Increasing the length of the wire', 'Decreasing the wire length', 'Increasing the current', 'Using a thicker wire', 'option1', 'Longer wire means smaller potential gradient (V/L), allowing detection of smaller potential differences.', 'p_exp_electricity', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a metre bridge, if the balance point is at $40\;\text{cm}$ with known resistance $R = 10\;\Omega$, the unknown resistance is', '$6.67\;\Omega$', '$15\;\Omega$', '$4\;\Omega$', '$25\;\Omega$', 'option1', '$X/R = l/(100-l) = 40/60 = 2/3$. $X = 10 \times 2/3 = 6.67\;\Omega$.', 'p_exp_electricity', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a potentiometer, the emf of a cell is balanced at $60\;\text{cm}$. If the potentiometer wire has potential gradient $0.02\;\text{V/cm}$, the emf is', '$0.12\;\text{V}$', '$0.6\;\text{V}$', '$3\;\text{V}$', '$1.2\;\text{V}$', 'option4', '$\varepsilon = kl = 0.02 \times 60 = 1.2\;\text{V}$.', 'p_exp_electricity', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The internal resistance of a cell can be measured using a potentiometer by', 'Measuring the current only', 'Comparing balance lengths with and without an external resistance', 'Using a voltmeter only', 'Heating the cell', 'option2', '$r = R(l_1/l_2 - 1)$ where $l_1$ is the balance length for emf and $l_2$ for terminal voltage with external $R$.', 'p_exp_electricity', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a Wheatstone bridge, the galvanometer is connected between', 'The battery terminals', 'The two midpoints of the bridge arms', 'In series with the battery', 'Across the known resistance', 'option2', 'The galvanometer connects between junctions B and D (the midpoints of the two branches).', 'p_exp_electricity', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ammeter should always be connected in', 'Series with the circuit element', 'Parallel', 'Either series or parallel', 'It does not matter', 'option1', 'An ammeter measures current flowing through it, so it must be in series.', 'p_exp_electricity', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A voltmeter should always be connected in', 'Series', 'Parallel with the component across which voltage is measured', 'Either way', 'In the ground wire', 'option2', 'A voltmeter measures potential difference across a component, so it must be in parallel.', 'p_exp_electricity', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Kelvin double bridge is used for measuring', 'Capacitance', 'Very high resistances', 'Very low resistances ($< 1\;\Omega$)', 'Inductance', 'option3', 'The Kelvin bridge eliminates lead and contact resistance errors, suitable for low-resistance measurements.', 'p_exp_electricity', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a potentiometer experiment, the driving cell must have emf', 'Zero', 'Less than the cell being measured', 'Equal to the cell being measured', 'Greater than the emf of the cell being measured', 'option4', 'The driving cell must have higher emf to ensure the potential drop across the wire exceeds the emf being measured.', 'p_exp_electricity', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('End corrections in a metre bridge can be eliminated by', 'Using a longer wire', 'Interchanging the resistances and averaging', 'Using a shorter wire', 'Increasing the current', 'option2', 'Interchanging $R$ and $X$ and averaging the two balance lengths eliminates end corrections.', 'p_exp_electricity', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The galvanometer in a Wheatstone bridge has maximum sensitivity when', 'The battery has zero internal resistance', 'One arm has very high resistance', 'The galvanometer resistance is zero', 'All four arms have equal resistance', 'option4', 'Maximum sensitivity when $P = Q = R = S$ (balanced with equal arms).', 'p_exp_electricity', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A potentiometer is preferred over a voltmeter for measuring emf because', 'It is cheaper', 'It draws no current from the cell at balance (null method)', 'It is more portable', 'It measures higher voltages', 'option2', 'At balance, no current flows through the cell, so there is no voltage drop across internal resistance. The true emf is measured.', 'p_exp_electricity', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a metre bridge, the percentage error in the unknown resistance is minimum when the balance point is at', '$75\;\text{cm}$', '$25\;\text{cm}$', '$50\;\text{cm}$', '$10\;\text{cm}$', 'option3', 'At $l = 50\;\text{cm}$, the percentage error $\Delta X/X = \Delta l/(l(100-l))$ is minimized.', 'p_exp_electricity', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The resistance of a galvanometer can be determined by', 'A calorimeter', 'Full-deflection method', 'Half-deflection method', 'A spectrometer', 'option3', 'Half-deflection method: with shunt $S$ giving half deflection, $G \approx S$ (for $S \ll R_{series}$).', 'p_exp_electricity', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a potentiometer, if the wire length is doubled (same total potential drop), the sensitivity', 'Halves', 'Doubles', 'Remains the same', 'Quadruples', 'option2', 'Potential gradient $= V/L$. Doubling $L$ halves the gradient, meaning smaller emfs can be balanced over longer lengths — sensitivity doubles.', 'p_exp_electricity', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

