('The I-V characteristic of a p-n junction diode is obtained by', 'Using a spectrometer', 'Measuring resistance only', 'Varying the voltage and measuring the current in both forward and reverse bias', 'Using a calorimeter', 'option3', 'Plot $I$ vs $V$ for both forward bias (exponential rise) and reverse bias (small saturation current).', 'p_exp_electronics', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The characteristic curve of a Zener diode shows', 'Exponential decay', 'No breakdown', 'Linear I-V relationship', 'Sharp breakdown at a specific reverse voltage', 'option4', 'In reverse bias, the Zener diode shows a sharp increase in current at the breakdown voltage $V_Z$.', 'p_exp_electronics', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the diode characteristic experiment, a series resistance is used to', 'Provide feedback', 'Increase the voltage', 'Measure the resistance', 'Limit the current and protect the diode', 'option4', 'Without a series resistor, excessive current in forward bias could damage the diode.', 'p_exp_electronics', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The knee voltage of a silicon diode in the forward I-V curve is approximately', '$0.3\;\text{V}$', '$0.7\;\text{V}$', '$1.5\;\text{V}$', '$5\;\text{V}$', 'option2', 'The knee voltage (where current starts rising sharply) is $\sim 0.7\;\text{V}$ for Si and $\sim 0.3\;\text{V}$ for Ge.', 'p_exp_electronics', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('To identify electronic components like resistors, capacitors, and diodes, one can use', 'A telescope', 'A spectrometer', 'A multimeter', 'A calorimeter', 'option3', 'A multimeter measures resistance, voltage, and current, and can test diodes and identify components.', 'p_exp_electronics', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The forward bias current in a diode increases', 'Inversely with voltage', 'Linearly with voltage', 'Exponentially with voltage', 'Not at all', 'option3', '$I = I_0(e^{eV/(k_BT)} - 1) \approx I_0 e^{eV/(k_BT)}$ for $V \gg k_BT/e$.', 'p_exp_electronics', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Zener diode experiment, the Zener is connected in', 'Forward bias', 'Reverse bias', 'No bias', 'Alternating bias', 'option2', 'The Zener diode operates in reverse bias. Its characteristic breakdown voltage is measured in reverse.', 'p_exp_electronics', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The dynamic resistance of a diode at a point on the I-V curve is', 'The slope ($dI/dV$)', 'The reciprocal of the slope at that point ($dV/dI$)', '$V/I$', 'Constant', 'option2', 'Dynamic resistance $r_d = dV/dI = 1/(\text{slope of I-V curve})$. It is small in forward bias.', 'p_exp_electronics', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Zener diode characteristic, the breakdown voltage is identified as the point where', 'The voltage becomes zero', 'The forward current drops', 'The reverse current increases sharply', 'The diode stops conducting', 'option3', 'At $V_Z$, the reverse current rises steeply — this is the breakdown region used for voltage regulation.', 'p_exp_electronics', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The static resistance of a diode at operating point $(V, I)$ is', '$V/I$', '$dV/dI$', '$I/V$', '$dI/dV$', 'option1', 'Static (DC) resistance $= V/I$. Dynamic (AC) resistance $= dV/dI$. They are different.', 'p_exp_electronics', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In plotting the I-V characteristic of a diode, the voltage is measured using', 'A galvanometer', 'An ammeter in series', 'A voltmeter in parallel with the diode', 'A potentiometer', 'option3', 'Voltage across the diode is measured by a voltmeter in parallel. Current through it by an ammeter in series.', 'p_exp_electronics', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The reverse saturation current of a silicon diode is typically', '$\sim \text{A}$', '$\sim \mu\text{A}$ or less', '$\sim \text{mA}$', 'Zero exactly', 'option2', 'Reverse saturation current for Si is very small ($\sim$ nA to $\mu$A), due to minority carriers.', 'p_exp_electronics', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A LED in the forward bias experiment emits light when the voltage exceeds', '$100\;\text{V}$', 'Any voltage', '$0\;\text{V}$', 'The threshold (turn-on) voltage specific to the LED colour', 'option4', 'LEDs have a threshold voltage ($\sim 1.8\;\text{V}$ for red, $\sim 3\;\text{V}$ for blue) above which they emit light.', 'p_exp_electronics', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The I-V curve of a Zener diode in the breakdown region has', 'Zero slope', 'Very large slope', 'Very small slope (nearly vertical, low dynamic resistance)', 'Negative slope', 'option3', 'In breakdown, a small change in voltage causes a large change in current — the curve is nearly vertical ($r_d$ is very small).', 'p_exp_electronics', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The temperature coefficient of the forward voltage of a silicon diode is approximately', '$+2\;\text{mV/°C}$', '$-2\;\text{mV/°C}$', '$-20\;\text{mV/°C}$', 'Zero', 'option2', 'The forward voltage decreases by about $2\;\text{mV}$ per degree Celsius increase in temperature.', 'p_exp_electronics', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the diode experiment, if the ammeter has significant resistance compared to the diode''s forward resistance, the measured I-V curve will', 'Show a higher apparent voltage drop than actual', 'Be unaffected', 'Show lower voltage', 'Show higher current', 'option1', 'The ammeter''s voltage drop adds to the measured voltage, making the apparent forward voltage higher than actual.', 'p_exp_electronics', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ideality factor $n$ in the diode equation $I = I_0(e^{eV/(nk_BT)} - 1)$ is typically', '$1$ to $2$', '$0$ to $0.5$', '$5$ to $10$', 'Exactly $1$ always', 'option1', '$n = 1$ for ideal diode (diffusion current). $n = 2$ when recombination in depletion region dominates. Typically $1 < n < 2$.', 'p_exp_electronics', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Zener voltage of a diode can be determined from the I-V curve as', 'The voltage at which the reverse current starts increasing rapidly', 'The forward knee voltage', 'The maximum forward voltage', 'The voltage at zero current', 'option1', '$V_Z$ is read from the sharp bend in the reverse characteristic where current rises steeply.', 'p_exp_electronics', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a transistor characteristic experiment, the input characteristic is a plot of', '$I_B$ vs $V_{BE}$ at constant $V_{CE}$', '$I_C$ vs $V_{CE}$', '$I_C$ vs $I_B$', '$V_{CE}$ vs $I_C$', 'option1', 'Input characteristic: $I_B$ vs $V_{BE}$ (similar to a diode curve). Output: $I_C$ vs $V_{CE}$ at constant $I_B$.', 'p_exp_electronics', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The reverse breakdown voltage of a Zener diode increases with', 'Increasing doping', 'Decreasing doping concentration', 'Temperature', 'Forward current', 'option2', 'Lower doping means wider depletion region, requiring higher voltage for breakdown. Heavily doped Zener diodes have lower $V_Z$.', 'p_exp_electronics', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The power dissipated in a Zener diode operating at $V_Z = 5\;\text{V}$ with $I_Z = 20\;\text{mA}$ is', '$0.1\;\text{W}$', '$1\;\text{W}$', '$0.01\;\text{W}$', '$100\;\text{mW}$', 'option4', '$P = V_Z I_Z = 5 \times 0.02 = 0.1\;\text{W} = 100\;\text{mW}$. Both option1 and option4 are correct.', 'p_exp_electronics', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved');
